OAI_PAPER({"slug": "The-Euclidean-plane-is-not-five-colorable-September-23-2026", "title": "The Euclidean plane is not five-colorable", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Euclidean-plane-is-not-five-colorable-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "158", "family_title": "The Euclidean plane cannot be colored with five colors", "abstract_html": "We prove that every coloring of the Euclidean plane with five colors has a monochromatic unit-distance pair, with no regularity assumption on the color classes. Consequently, the chromatic number of the plane is either six or seven.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The Euclidean plane has no proper five-coloring.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work in ZFC.</p><p class=\"li\">A proper \\(k\\)-coloring of the Euclidean plane is a function\n  \\[\n  c:\\mathbb R^2\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\ldots,k\\}\n  \\]\n  satisfying \\(c(x)\\ne c(y)\\) whenever \\(\\lVert x-y\\rVert=1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">No regularity is imposed on the color classes.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\chi(\\mathbb R^2)\\) denote the least number of colors in such a coloring.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The Euclidean plane has no proper five-coloring. Consequently,\n\\[\n6\\le \\chi(\\mathbb R^2)\\le7.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>A proper five-coloring would give a weak measurable five-coloring by Proposition 2, contrary to Proposition 16. Thus \\(\\chi(\\mathbb R^2)\\ge6\\).</p><p>For the upper bound, take Voronoi hexagons of a triangular lattice, each of circumradius \\(r=2/5\\). Their centers have nearest-neighbor distance \\(\\sqrt3r\\). Write the lattice as a scaled copy of \\(\\mathbb Z+\\mathbb Z\\omega\\), where \\(\\omega=e^{2\\pi i/3}\\). Multiplication by \\(2-\\omega\\) gives a similar sublattice of index seven, since \\(|2-\\omega|^2=7\\).</p><p>Color centers by the seven cosets and each point by its hexagon, assigning boundaries to any incident hexagon. Within one hexagon distances are at most \\(2r&lt;1\\). Distinct same-color centers are at least \\(\\sqrt{21}r\\) apart; their hexagons therefore have point distances at least\n\\[\n(\\sqrt{21}-2)r&gt;1.\n\\]\nThis is a proper seven-coloring, including boundaries.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Theorem 1.3, Transfer of colorability", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\text{A proper \\(k\\)-coloring of \\(\\mathbb R^2\\) exists} \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\text{a weak measurable \\(k\\)-coloring of \\(\\mathbb R^2\\) exists}.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work in ZFC, and let \\(k\\) be a positive integer.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S^1\\subset\\mathbb R^2\\) be the unit circle and let \\(\\sigma\\) be normalized arc-length measure.</p><p class=\"li\">As in original Definition 1.2, a weak measurable \\(k\\)-coloring is a Lebesgue-measurable function\n  \\[\n  c:\\mathbb R^2\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\ldots,k\\},\n  \\qquad A_i=c^{-1}(\\{i\\}),\n  \\]\n  such that, for every \\(R&gt;0\\),\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{i=1}^k\n  \\int_{B(0,R)}\\int_{S^1}\n  1_{A_i}(x)1_{A_i}(x+u)\\,d\\sigma(u)\\,dx=0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The integrals use the completed product measure. Equivalently, choose Borel representatives of the color classes forming a partition of the plane.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\text{A proper \\(k\\)-coloring of \\(\\mathbb R^2\\) exists}\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n\\text{a weak measurable \\(k\\)-coloring of \\(\\mathbb R^2\\) exists}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 2 proves the forward implication. Proposition 3 proves the reverse implication by density-point properness, common translations of finite configurations, and graph compactness in ZFC.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "Corollary 1.5, Positive five-color badness", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a constant \\(\\delta>0\\) such that:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Identify \\(\\mathbb R^2\\) with \\(\\mathbb C\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E(2)\\) denote the plane-isometry group.</p><p class=\"li\">For a coloring \\(c:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\{1,\\ldots,5\\}\\) and a normalized finitely additive left-invariant measure \\(\\mu\\) on all subsets of \\(E(2)\\), define\n  \\[\n  p_5^\\mu(c)=\n  \\mu\\bigl(\\{T\\in E(2):c(T^{-1}0)=c(T^{-1}1)\\}\\bigr).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a constant \\(\\delta&gt;0\\) such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. For every such coloring \\(c\\) and every such measure \\(\\mu\\),\n   \\[\n   p_5^\\mu(c)\\ge\\delta.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If \\(c\\) is a Lebesgue-measurable five-coloring with two linearly independent periods, \\(A\\) is uniform in a fundamental parallelogram, and \\(U\\) is an independent uniform unit direction, then\n   \\[\n   \\mathbb P(c(A)=c(A+U))\\ge\\delta.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. For any Lebesgue-measurable five-coloring, if\n   \\[\n   \\mathbb P\\bigl(c(A_R)=c(A_R+U)\\mid A_R+U\\in[-R,R]^2\\bigr),\n   \\qquad R&gt;1,\n   \\]\n   converges as \\(R\\to\\infty\\), its limit is at least \\(\\delta\\). Here \\(A_R\\) is uniform in \\([-R,R]^2\\) and independent of \\(U\\).</p><p>The graph and positive constant obtained in the proof are existential.</p>", "proof": "<p>Theorem 1 and graph compactness give a finite unit-distance graph \\(H\\) that is not five-colorable. Set\n\\[\nm=|E(H)|,\\qquad\\delta=1/m.\n\\]\nEvery five-coloring of \\(H\\) has at least one monochromatic edge. Average this count using any measure in the first assertion. Isometry invariance and finite additivity give\n\\[\nmp_5^\\mu(c)\\ge1.\n\\]\nThis is the finite-graph badness bound cited in the paper from Gwyn and Stavrianos.</p><p>For periodic and convergent asymptotic needle frequencies, the finite-graph averaging results of Bourgeat et al., cited in the paper as Theorems 1–2, bound those frequencies below by the minimum monochromatic-edge fraction of \\(H\\), hence by \\(1/m\\).</p><p>Borel representatives have the same needle frequencies by Fubini. In the periodic case choose them in one fundamental cell and extend periodically. This proves all assertions with the existential constant \\(\\delta\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Theorem 2.3, Rigidity of wild character laws", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every wild \\(K\\)-invariant probability on \\(D\\) is normalized Haar probability:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">As in original Definition 2.1, put\n  \\[\n  F=\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\cap\\mathbb R,\\qquad\n  E=F(i),\\qquad\n  K=\\{u\\in E:u\\bar u=1\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Regard \\(E\\) as an additive discrete group and \\(K\\) as a multiplicative discrete group.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  D=\\widehat{E_{\\mathrm{disc}}}\n  \\]\n  be the compact character group. Use additive notation on \\(D\\):\n  \\[\n  (d+d')(z)=d(z)d'(z).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(a,z\\in E\\), define\n  \\[\n  (ad)(z)=d(az),\\qquad \\psi_z(d)=d(z).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define the subgroup of ordinary continuous characters by\n  \\[\n  C=j(\\mathbb R^2),\\qquad\n  j(\\xi)(z)=\\exp(i\\xi\\cdot z).\n  \\]\n  The map \\(j\\) is continuous and injective. Compact exhaustion of \\(\\mathbb R^2\\) shows that \\(C\\) is Borel and \\(j^{-1}:C\\to\\mathbb R^2\\) is Borel.</p><p class=\"li\">Multiplication by each nonzero \\(a\\in E\\) is an automorphism of \\(D\\) carrying \\(C\\) onto itself. Elements of \\(K\\) act orthogonally on frequency parameters.</p><p class=\"li\">Both \\(E\\) and \\(K\\) are countable abelian groups. Every nonzero power map on \\(K\\) is onto, so \\(K\\) is divisible.</p><p class=\"li\">As in original Definition 2.2, a \\(K\\)-invariant Borel probability \\(\\nu\\) on \\(D\\) is wild if \\(\\nu(C)=0\\). Write\n  \\[\n  f_\\nu(z)=\\int_D\\psi_z(d)\\,d\\nu(d).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every wild \\(K\\)-invariant probability on \\(D\\) is normalized Haar probability:\n\\[\n\\nu(C)=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\nu=m_D.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>First let \\(\\mu\\) be ergodic and wild, and use \\(Y,m,w\\) from Lemma 1.7. Suppose \\(m(r)&gt;0\\) for some positive \\(r\\in F\\). Choose finite \\(H&gt;0\\) with \\(e^{-H}&lt;m(r)^2\\).</p><p>The set\n\\[\nA_0=\\{u\\in K:w(2r|\\operatorname{Re}u|)\\le H\\}\n\\]\nis syndetic. Otherwise every finite \\(S\\subset K\\) has a translate avoiding \\(A_0\\). Translating each set of a Følner sequence this way gives another Følner sequence with all averaged \\(m\\)-values at most \\(e^{-H}\\), contradicting Lemma 1.7. Hence there are \\(q_1,\\ldots,q_J\\in K\\) such that for every \\(v\\in K\\), some \\(q_jv\\in A_0\\).</p><p>Choose a number field \\(N_0\\subset F\\) containing real and imaginary parts of all \\(q_j\\), and put \\(N=N_0(i)\\), \\(d_0=[N:\\mathbb Q]\\). By Lemma 1.8, choose a general-position set \\(S\\subset K\\cap N\\) of size greater than \\(J(d_0-1)\\). For every \\(v\\), pigeonhole gives \\(d_0\\) elements \\(s\\in S\\) and one \\(j\\) with \\(q_jsv\\in A_0\\). Their coefficients \\(k=q_js\\) form a \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-basis \\(T\\) of \\(N\\), called a good selection.</p><p>There are finitely many possible selections. Clear denominators simultaneously to obtain \\(D_0\\in\\mathbb N\\), \\(M_0&lt;\\infty\\), such that for each selection\n\\[\nD_0\\,1=\\sum_{k\\in T}a_kk,\\qquad\na_k\\in\\mathbb Z,\\qquad\\sum_{k\\in T}|a_k|\\le M_0.\n\\]\nThere is a common \\(0&lt;c_0\\le1\\) with\n\\[\n\\max_{k\\in T}|\\operatorname{Re}(kv)|\\ge c_0\n\\qquad(v\\in\\mathbb T).\n\\]\nFor a fixed basis this continuous maximum never vanishes: its rational span contains \\(1,i\\), so vanishing would force both \\(\\operatorname{Re}v\\) and \\(\\operatorname{Re}(iv)\\) to vanish. Take minima on the circle and over the finite selections.</p><p>For \\(v\\in K\\) with \\(|\\operatorname{Re}v|&lt;c_0/D_0\\), take a good selection and put\n\\[\np_k=2r|\\operatorname{Re}(kv)|,\\qquad p=\\max_kp_k.\n\\]\nThen \\(w(p_k)\\le H\\) and \\(p\\ge2rc_0\\). Taking real parts of the basis identity, and inserting signs, expresses\n\\[\nz=2rD_0|\\operatorname{Re}v|&lt;p\n\\]\nas an integer combination of the \\(p_k\\) with coefficient length at most \\(M_0\\). Lemma 1.9 gives\n\\[\nw(2rD_0|\\operatorname{Re}v|)\\le4M_0H.\n\\]</p><p>Every \\(t\\in F\\cap[-1,1]\\) is the real part of \\(t+i\\sqrt{1-t^2}\\in K\\). Thus\n\\[\nm(s)\\ge e^{-4M_0H}&gt;0\n\\qquad(s\\in F\\cap(0,2rc_0)).\n\\]\nChoose nonnegative \\(\\phi\\in C_c((0,2rc_0))\\) of positive integral. Positivity gives\n\\[\nI(\\phi(t)m(t))\n\\ge e^{-4M_0H}\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\phi(t)\\,dt&gt;0,\n\\]\ncontrary to Lemma 1.2 for the wild law \\(\\nu_Y\\). Hence \\(m(z)=0\\) for every nonzero \\(z\\in E\\), and\n\\[\n\\left|\\int_D\\psi_z\\,d\\mu\\right|\n=\\left|\\int_Yg_z\\,d\\nu\\right|\n\\le\\|g_z\\|_2=0.\n\\]\nFourier uniqueness identifies \\(\\mu=m_D\\).</p><p>For arbitrary wild invariant \\(\\nu\\), use ergodic decomposition for the countable group on the standard compact space \\(D\\). Since \\(C\\) is invariant Borel and null, almost every component is wild, hence Haar by the ergodic case. Their integral is Haar.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every coset of \\(C\\) has \\(\\nu\\)-measure zero.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(F,E,K,D,C,\\psi_z\\) from Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\nu\\) be wild.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every coset of \\(C\\) has \\(\\nu\\)-measure zero. The law of the difference of two independent \\(\\nu\\)-samples gives \\(C\\) measure zero.</p><p>Moreover, for every \\(a\\in E\\setminus\\{0\\}\\), the event\n\\[\nL_a=\\{d\\in D:t\\mapsto d(at)\\text{ is continuous on \\(F\\) in its usual topology}\\}\n\\]\nis Borel and has \\(\\nu\\)-measure zero.</p>", "proof": "<p>If a coset \\(d+C\\) has positive mass, its \\(K\\)-orbit is finite, since the orbit cosets are disjoint and have equal mass. The action on this finite orbit gives a finite image of the divisible abelian group \\(K\\), which must be trivial. Hence\n\\[\n(u-1)d\\in C\\qquad(u\\in K).\n\\]\nTaking \\(u\\ne1\\) and applying the inverse scalar shows \\(d\\in C\\), contrary to \\(\\nu(C)=0\\).</p><p>The relation \\(d'-d\\in C\\) is Borel. Integrating \\(\\nu(d+C)=0\\) against \\(\\nu(d)\\) proves the difference assertion.</p><p>Restriction to \\(aF\\) is continuous from \\(D\\) to \\(\\widehat{F_{\\mathrm{disc}}}\\). The ordinary continuous characters of \\(F\\) are exactly \\(t\\mapsto e^{i\\lambda t}\\), \\(\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R\\); their parametrization has Borel image by compact exhaustion. Thus \\(L_a\\) is Borel.</p><p>If \\(aF,bF\\) are nonparallel, then \\(a,b\\) form an \\(F\\)-basis of \\(E\\). A character continuous on both lines has the form\n\\[\nd(at+bs)=e^{i(\\lambda t+\\eta s)},\\qquad t,s\\in F,\n\\]\nand therefore belongs to \\(C\\). Thus \\(L_a\\cap L_b\\subset C\\). Rotations produce infinitely many distinct unoriented lines \\(uaF\\), with equal-measure events pairwise disjoint modulo null sets. Their common measure is zero.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma 2.5, Vanishing line average", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( I\\bigl(\\phi(t)f_\\nu(at)\\bigr)=0 \\qquad (a\\in E\\setminus\\{0\\},\\ \\phi\\in C_c(\\mathbb R)). \\) No ordinary real-variable measurability of \\(t\\mapsto f_\\nu(at)\\) is assumed.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the setting of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a translation-invariant mean \\(m\\) on all bounded functions on the discrete abelian group \\(F/\\mathbb Z\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a bounded function \\(h:F\\to\\mathbb C\\) supported in a bounded interval, define\n  \\[\n  I(h)=m\\left(t+\\mathbb Z\\mapsto\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z}h(t+k)\\right).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The functional \\(I\\) is positive and translation invariant under \\(F\\). For \\(\\phi\\in C_c(\\mathbb R)\\),\n  \\[\n  I(\\phi|_F)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\phi(t)\\,dt.\n  \\]\n  Indeed, the mean on continuous periodic functions is ordinary circle integration, by vanishing on nonconstant circle characters and trigonometric approximation.</p><p class=\"li\">For bounded, boundedly supported Hilbert-space-valued functions, interpret \\(I\\) weakly by Hilbert-space duality; then\n  \\[\n  \\|I(V)\\|\\le I(\\|V(t)\\|).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\nu\\) be wild.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nI\\bigl(\\phi(t)f_\\nu(at)\\bigr)=0\n\\qquad\n(a\\in E\\setminus\\{0\\},\\ \\phi\\in C_c(\\mathbb R)).\n\\]\nNo ordinary real-variable measurability of \\(t\\mapsto f_\\nu(at)\\) is assumed.</p>", "proof": "<p>On \\(L^2(\\nu)\\), consider the representation of the additive group \\(F\\)\n\\[\nT_tv=\\psi_{at}v.\n\\]\nIt has no nonzero vector strongly continuous for the usual topology of \\(F\\). For if \\(v\\) were such a vector, then\n\\[\nc_v(t)=\\int_D|v|^2\\psi_{at}\\,d\\nu\n\\]\nwould be positive definite and uniformly continuous, since\n\\[\n|c_v(t+s)-c_v(t)|\n\\le\\|v\\|_2\\|T_sv-v\\|_2.\n\\]\nIt extends to a continuous positive-definite function on \\(\\mathbb R\\); positive definiteness follows by approximation of each finite set of arguments.</p><p>Bochner's theorem on \\(\\mathbb R\\) supplies a finite positive representing measure. Push it to \\(\\widehat{F_{\\mathrm{disc}}}\\) by the ordinary-character parametrization. Its Fourier coefficients agree with the restriction-map pushforward of \\(|v|^2\\nu\\). Fourier uniqueness on the compact character group identifies the measures: characters form a unital conjugation-closed point-separating algebra, and Stone–Weierstrass gives uniform density. Consequently \\(|v|^2\\nu\\) is concentrated on \\(L_a\\). Lemma 1.1 gives \\(v=0\\).</p><p>Now form\n\\[\nV_\\phi=I\\bigl(\\phi(t)T_t1\\bigr)\\in L^2(\\nu).\n\\]\nTranslation invariance gives\n\\[\nT_sV_\\phi-V_\\phi\n=I\\bigl((\\phi(t-s)-\\phi(t))T_t1\\bigr),\n\\]\nand hence\n\\[\n\\|T_sV_\\phi-V_\\phi\\|_2\n\\le I(|\\phi(\\cdot-s)-\\phi|)\n=\\int_{\\mathbb R}|\\phi(t-s)-\\phi(t)|\\,dt\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nThus \\(V_\\phi\\) is a continuous vector and is zero. Pairing with the constant function proves the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "Lemma 2.6", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The function \\(J_\\lambda\\) has a unique minimizer \\(L(\\lambda)\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\lambda\\) be any Borel probability on \\(\\mathbb R^2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  J_\\lambda(s)=\n  \\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\n  \\left(\\sqrt{1+|s-t|^2}-\\sqrt{1+|t|^2}\\right)\\,d\\lambda(t).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The function \\(J_\\lambda\\) has a unique minimizer \\(L(\\lambda)\\). The map \\(\\lambda\\mapsto L(\\lambda)\\) is Borel for the usual Borel structure on probability measures, and is equivariant under translations and orthogonal maps.</p>", "proof": "<p>The integrand is bounded in absolute value by \\(|s|\\), so no first-moment assumption is needed. The objective is Lipschitz and strictly convex in \\(s\\).</p><p>Choose \\(R&gt;0\\) with \\(p=\\lambda(B(0,R))&gt;1/2\\). On this ball the integrand is at least\n\\[\n|s|-R-\\sqrt{1+R^2},\n\\]\nand elsewhere it is at least \\(-|s|\\). Therefore\n\\[\nJ_\\lambda(s)\\ge\n(2p-1)|s|-p\\bigl(R+\\sqrt{1+R^2}\\bigr)\\longrightarrow\\infty.\n\\]\nCoercivity and continuity give existence, and strict convexity gives uniqueness.</p><p>For fixed \\(s\\), the integrand is bounded and continuous in \\(t\\), so \\(\\lambda\\mapsto J_\\lambda(s)\\) is Borel. Enumerate \\(\\mathbb Q^2\\); for each \\(n\\), choose its first point with objective less than the rational-point infimum plus \\(1/n\\). These choices are measurable and converge to the unique minimizer: coercivity confines accurate choices to a compact set, and every cluster point minimizes. Their limit \\(L\\) is Borel.</p><p>If \\(\\lambda'\\) is the law of \\(t-r\\), then\n\\[\nJ_{\\lambda'}(s)=J_\\lambda(s+r)-J_\\lambda(r),\n\\]\nso \\(L(\\lambda')=L(\\lambda)-r\\). For an orthogonal map \\(O\\),\n\\[\nJ_{O_*\\lambda}(Os)=J_\\lambda(s).\n\\]\nThese identities prove the equivariances.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.4", "orig": "Lemma 2.7, Relative singularity", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every factor \\(Y\\) in the tower, the law \\(\\nu_Y\\) of \\( d(x)-d(x') \\) for two conditionally independent samples over \\(Y\\) satisfies \\( \\nu_Y(C)=0. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(K,D,C\\) from Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X\\) be an ergodic standard probability-preserving \\(K\\)-system and let \\(d:X\\to D\\) be equivariant with wild law.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(U_kH(x)=H(kx)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Factors are countably generated modulo null sets. Conditional kernels and equivariance identities are chosen on common conull sets, using countability of \\(K\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\((Y_\\alpha)_{\\alpha\\le\\theta}\\) be a tower of factors, where \\(\\theta\\) is countable, \\(Y_0\\) is trivial, and limit stages are generated joins.</p><p class=\"li\">Require at every successor \\(Y\\) over its predecessor \\(W\\): for every bounded \\(H\\) on \\(Y\\) and \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), there are fixed bounded \\(H_1,\\ldots,H_q\\) on \\(Y\\) and \\(B&lt;\\infty\\) such that, for every \\(k\\in K\\),\n  \\[\n  \\left\\|U_kH-\\sum_{r=1}^q b_r^kH_r\\right\\|_2&lt;\\varepsilon\n  \\]\n  for some \\(b_r^k\\in L^\\infty(W)\\) with \\(\\|b_r^k\\|_\\infty\\le B\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every factor \\(Y\\) in the tower, the law \\(\\nu_Y\\) of\n\\[\nd(x)-d(x')\n\\]\nfor two conditionally independent samples over \\(Y\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\nu_Y(C)=0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>All the relative difference laws are \\(K\\)-invariant by equivariance of conditional kernels. Use transfinite induction universally over all ambient systems, maps, and towers satisfying the stated hypotheses. This allows earlier induction assertions to be applied to new factor systems and maps. At the trivial factor, Lemma 1.1 applies.</p><p>Suppose the assertion fails at a noninitial stage \\(Y\\), and let \\(\\kappa_y\\) be the conditional law of \\(d\\) over \\(Y\\). The function\n\\[\nr(y,z)=\\kappa_y(z+C)\n=\\int_D1_C(d'-z)\\,d\\kappa_y(d')\n\\]\nis Borel by parameter integration. The function \\(x\\mapsto r(y(x),d(x))\\) is invariant and has integral \\(\\nu_Y(C)&gt;0\\). Ergodicity makes it a positive constant \\(a\\) almost everywhere. Thus almost every \\(\\kappa_y\\) is concentrated on \\(m\\) distinct \\(C\\)-cosets, each of mass \\(1/m=a\\), where \\(m\\) is independent of \\(y\\). None is \\(C\\), since the marginal law is wild.</p><p>On \\(r(y,z)&gt;0\\), define\n\\[\n\\lambda_{y,z}(A)=\n\\frac1{r(y,z)}\n\\int_D1_C(d'-z)\\,\n1_A\\bigl(j^{-1}(d'-z)\\bigr)\\,d\\kappa_y(d').\n\\]\nThe integrand is interpreted as zero off \\(C\\). Set\n\\[\nb(y,z)=z+j\\bigl(L(\\lambda_{y,z})\\bigr).\n\\]\nLemma 1.3 shows that this is constant on each positive-mass coset, stays in that coset, and satisfies\n\\[\nb(ky,kz)=kb(y,z).\n\\]\nLet \\(Z\\) be the factor generated by \\(Y\\) and \\(b(x)=b(y(x),d(x))\\). It is ergodic and standard. The map \\(b:Z\\to D\\) has wild law, and its conditional law \\(\\beta_y\\) over \\(Y\\) is uniform on \\(m\\) distinct centers.</p><p>Encode the unordered list of \\(m\\) values of \\(\\psi_1\\) on these centers by its elementary symmetric functions \\(H(y)\\in\\mathbb C^m\\). This is measurable, because its power sums are\n\\[\nm\\int_D\\psi_1^n\\,d\\beta_y\n\\]\nand Newton's identities recover the elementary symmetric functions. The encoding is a continuous injection of the compact space \\(\\mathbb T^m/S_m\\). Its inverse is uniformly continuous for\n\\[\nd_{\\rm match}([z_r],[z'_r])\n=\\min_{\\pi\\in S_m}\\max_r|z_r-z'_{\\pi(r)}|.\n\\]\nEquivariance makes \\(U_kH\\) encode the phase list at \\(\\psi_k\\).</p><p>Choose \\(\\rho&gt;0\\) so that uniform-circle probability of \\(\\{|z-1|\\le5\\rho\\}\\) is less than \\(1/(16m^2)\\). Continuous majorization of the \\(4\\rho\\)-arc and uniform trigonometric approximation give \\(N\\ge1\\) and \\(c&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\n\\left|\\int z^n\\,d\\omega(z)\\right|&lt;c\\quad(1\\le n\\le N)\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\omega\\{|z-1|\\le4\\rho\\}&lt;\\frac1{4m^2}.\n\\]\nSpecifically, approximate the majorant within \\(1/(64m^2)\\), and choose \\(c\\) so that its product with the sum of absolute nonconstant coefficients is less than \\(1/(32m^2)\\). Negative moments are controlled by positive moments.</p><p>Choose \\(s&gt;0\\) so that encoding distance less than \\(s\\) gives matching distance less than \\(\\rho\\). Put \\(\\tau=s/100\\), and choose \\(\\gamma&gt;0\\) with\n\\[\n2\\gamma^2/\\tau^2&lt;10^{-4}.\n\\]\nFor some earlier factor \\(W\\), the rotations of \\(H\\) have approximations\n\\[\n\\left\\|U_kH-\\sum_{r=1}^q b_r^kH_r\\right\\|_2&lt;\\gamma,\n\\qquad \\|b_r^k\\|_\\infty\\le B,\n\\]\nwith fixed bounded vector-valued \\(H_r\\). At a successor this is the tower property. At a limit, first approximate \\(H\\) within \\(\\gamma/2\\) on an earlier successor factor and then apply its successor property within \\(\\gamma/2\\).</p><p>The earlier tower through \\(W\\) is also a tower in \\(Z\\). Universal induction applied to \\(b\\) shows that its difference law over \\(W\\) is wild. Its Fourier coefficient is\n\\[\np(a)=\\|\\mathbb E_Z(\\psi_a(b)\\mid W)\\|_2^2\\ge0.\n\\]\nLemma 1.2 applies to \\(p\\).</p><p>At each fixed \\(W\\)-fiber, partition the bounded set of pairs of coefficient arrays into \\(L&lt;\\infty\\) bins so fine that approximants in one bin differ pointwise by at most \\(\\tau\\) for each vector. Coordinate diameters less than\n\\[\n\\frac{\\tau}{1+\\sum_r\\|H_r\\|_\\infty}\n\\]\nsuffice. Fix \\(M\\ge2\\) with\n\\[\n0.9M/L&gt;10m^2.\n\\]</p><p>There is \\(0\\ne t\\in F\\), \\(|t|&lt;1/M\\), for which all the finitely many values\n\\[\np(n(j-l)t),\\qquad\n1\\le n\\le N,\\quad 1\\le j,l\\le M,\\quad j\\ne l,\n\\]\nare as small as prescribed. Indeed, choose nonnegative\n\\(\\phi\\in C_c((-1/M,1/M))\\) of positive integral. Each nonnegative test has zero \\(\\phi\\)-weighted \\(I\\)-integral by Lemma 1.2. Positivity prevents their finite sum from being bounded below by any prescribed positive number throughout \\(\\{\\phi&gt;0\\}\\). Smallness below one excludes \\(t=0\\).</p><p>Put \\(x_j=jt\\). Choose this smallness so that Markov's inequality and a union bound give, on a set of \\(W\\)-fibers of probability greater than \\(0.99\\),\n\\[\n|\\mathbb E_Z(\\psi_{n(x_j-x_l)}(b)\\mid W=w)|&lt;c\n\\qquad(1\\le n\\le N,\\ j&lt;l).\n\\]\nThe failure probability for each bound is at most \\(p(n(x_j-x_l))/c^2\\). The circle-moment implication therefore gives simultaneously\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_Z\\bigl(\n|\\psi_{x_j}(b)-\\psi_{x_l}(b)|\\le4\\rho\\mid W=w\n\\bigr)&lt;\\frac1{4m^2}.\n\\tag{*}\n\\]</p><p>Write\n\\[\nk_j=\\frac{x_j}{2}+i\\sqrt{1-\\frac{x_j^2}{4}},\n\\qquad\nk'_j=\\frac{x_j}{2}-i\\sqrt{1-\\frac{x_j^2}{4}}.\n\\]\nThen \\(k_j,k'_j\\in K\\) and \\(x_j=k_j+k'_j\\). Apply the fixed approximations to \\(U_{k_j}H,U_{k'_j}H\\). Their conditional squared errors have integrals less than \\(\\gamma^2\\). If at least \\(0.1M\\) indices have one conditional \\(L^2\\)-error at least \\(\\tau\\), the mean squared error is at least \\(0.1\\tau^2\\). Markov bounds this event by\n\\[\n20\\gamma^2/\\tau^2&lt;0.001.\n\\]\nHence on more than \\(0.99\\) of the fibers, more than \\(0.9M\\) indices have both errors less than \\(\\tau\\).</p><p>Fix a fiber with this property and (*). A single bin contains a set \\(J\\) of good indices with \\(|J|&gt;10m^2\\). Fix \\(j_0\\in J\\). For either encoding, the conditional \\(L^2\\)-distance from index \\(j\\) to \\(j_0\\) is at most \\(3\\tau\\). For uniform \\(j\\in J\\) and conditional \\(y\\), the probability that either encoding distance is at least \\(s\\) is at most\n\\[\n18\\tau^2/s^2=0.0018.\n\\]\nIf \\(q(y)\\) is the fraction of indices for which both phase lists match within \\(\\rho\\), then\n\\[\n\\mathbb E(q\\mid W=w)&gt;0.95.\n\\]</p><p>For any center \\(b_0\\) in the support of \\(\\beta_y\\), every matching index satisfies\n\\[\n\\psi_{x_j}(b_0)=\\psi_{k_j}(b_0)\\psi_{k'_j}(b_0),\n\\]\nwhich is within \\(2\\rho\\) of one of at most \\(m^2\\) products from the representative lists. Assign matching indices to such products. The sum-of-squares inequality gives at least \\(q(y)^2|J|^2/m^2\\) ordered pairs in the same bin. Removing diagonals discards at most \\(|J|\\) pairs. Thus\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_{j,l}\\bigl(j\\ne l,\\\n|\\psi_{x_j}(b_0)-\\psi_{x_l}(b_0)|\\le4\\rho\\bigr)\n\\ge\\frac{q(y)^2}{m^2}-\\frac1{|J|}.\n\\]\nIntegrate over \\(\\beta_y\\) and then over \\(y\\) conditioned on \\(w\\). Jensen gives a lower bound greater than\n\\[\n\\frac{0.95^2}{m^2}-\\frac1{|J|}\n&gt;\n\\frac{0.95^2-0.1}{m^2}\n&gt;\n\\frac1{4m^2}.\n\\]\nThis contradicts the simultaneous upper bounds (*) for all distinct pairs. Choosing \\(J\\) after fixing \\(w\\) is harmless because those bounds hold simultaneously. The induction is complete.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.5", "orig": "Lemma 3.1, Compact tower", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a countable ordinal \\(\\eta\\) and an increasing factor tower \\((Y_\\alpha)_{\\alpha\\le\\eta}\\) such that:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X\\) be an ergodic standard probability-preserving \\(K\\)-system.</p><p class=\"li\">For a factor map \\(X\\to Y\\), disintegrate \\(\\mu=\\int\\mu_y\\,d\\nu(y)\\) and give the relative product \\(X\\times_YX\\) measure\n  \\[\n  \\mu\\times_Y\\mu=\\int\\mu_y\\otimes\\mu_y\\,d\\nu(y).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use the successor approximation property specified in Lemma 1.4.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a countable ordinal \\(\\eta\\) and an increasing factor tower \\((Y_\\alpha)_{\\alpha\\le\\eta}\\) such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(Y_0\\) is trivial, and limit stages are generated joins;</p><p class=\"li num\">2. every successor satisfies the stated approximation property;</p><p class=\"li num\">3. \\(X\\times_YX\\) is ergodic for \\(Y=Y_\\eta\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(X\\times_YX\\) is not ergodic, choose a bounded invariant nonconstant kernel \\(L(x,x')\\). Its conditional expectations onto either coordinate are invariant on \\(X\\), hence equal its integral. Subtract that constant. The remaining nonzero kernel has both coordinate conditional expectations zero.</p><p>On \\(H_y=L^2(\\mu_y)\\), let \\(A_y\\) be the corresponding Hilbert–Schmidt operator. Then\n\\[\n\\|A_y\\|_{\\rm HS}\\le\\|L\\|_\\infty,\n\\qquad A_y1=A_y^*1=0.\n\\]\nFor sufficiently small fixed \\(\\delta&gt;0\\),\n\\[\nQ_y=1_{(\\delta,\\infty)}(A_y^*A_y)\n\\]\nis nonzero on a positive-measure set. Its finite rank is bounded by \\(\\|L\\|_\\infty^2/\\delta\\), is invariant on the ergodic factor \\(Y\\), and therefore equals a constant \\(d\\ge1\\) almost everywhere. Its range is orthogonal to fiber constants.</p><p>For measurability, choose simple functions with complex-rational coefficients from a countable generating algebra of \\(X\\). Their fiber restrictions are dense. Conditional integration makes their images under \\(A_y,A_y^*\\), and polynomials in \\(A_y^*A_y\\), measurable. Uniformly bounded polynomial approximations converging pointwise to \\(1_{(\\delta,\\infty)}\\), with value zero at \\(\\delta\\), give measurable projected sections by spectral calculus and dominated convergence. Measurable independence tests and fiberwise Gram–Schmidt yield fiber-orthonormal measurable sections \\(e_1,\\ldots,e_d\\).</p><p>Rotations act on these sections through unitary coefficient matrices over \\(Y\\). Thus \\(\\sum_i|e_i(x)|^2\\) is invariant on \\(X\\), and its integral is \\(d\\). Ergodicity gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^d|e_i(x)|^2=d\n\\quad\\text{almost everywhere}.\n\\]\nThe sections are bounded. Adjoin them to \\(Y\\), producing an invariant strict extension \\(Y'\\): their conditional means vanish, whereas their fiber norms are one.</p><p>Bounded polynomials in \\(e_i,\\bar e_i\\), with bounded coefficients over \\(Y\\), are dense in \\(L^2(Y')\\). A rotation of a fixed polynomial expands into finitely many fixed bounded monomials with coefficients over \\(Y\\), uniformly bounded independently of the rotation. Approximate any bounded \\(H\\) by one polynomial. Rotation preserves its error, proving the successor approximation property.</p><p>Iterate strict enlargement when the relative product is not ergodic, and take joins at limits. The process stops at a countable ordinal. Otherwise the successive strict extensions through all countable ordinals provide \\(\\aleph_1\\) pairwise orthogonal unit vectors in the separable space \\(L^2(X)\\), a contradiction. The stopping rule gives the final ergodicity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.6", "orig": "Lemma 3.2, Conditional multiple averages", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For pairwise distinct integers \\(n_1,\\ldots,n_l\\) and bounded \\(v_1,\\ldots,v_l\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X\\) be an ergodic standard probability-preserving \\(K\\)-system.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Y\\) be invariant and suppose \\(X\\times_YX\\) is ergodic.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(P=\\mathbb E(\\,\\cdot\\mid Y)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A Følner sequence consists of nonempty finite \\(\\Phi_N\\subset K\\) satisfying\n  \\[\n  |h\\Phi_N\\triangle\\Phi_N|/|\\Phi_N|\\longrightarrow0\n  \\qquad(h\\in K).\n  \\]\n  Write \\(\\operatorname{Avg}_{u\\in\\Phi}b_u=|\\Phi|^{-1}\\sum_{u\\in\\Phi}b_u\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For pairwise distinct integers \\(n_1,\\ldots,n_l\\) and bounded \\(v_1,\\ldots,v_l\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Avg}_{u\\in\\Phi_N}\n\\left\\|\nP\\left(\\prod_{j=1}^lU_{u^{n_j}}v_j\\right)\n-\\prod_{j=1}^lU_{u^{n_j}}Pv_j\n\\right\\|_2^2\\longrightarrow0\n\\]\nalong every Følner sequence.</p><p>The pair criterion used in the proof is, for bounded \\(v,w\\) and \\(d\\ne0\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Avg}_{u\\in\\Phi_N}\n\\|P(vU_{u^d}w)-Pv\\,U_{u^d}Pw\\|_2^2\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The mean ergodic theorem holds along every Følner sequence: the orthogonal complement of invariant vectors is the closed span of \\(V_hv-v\\), whose averages have norm at most\n\\[\n|h\\Phi_N\\triangle\\Phi_N|\\|v\\|/|\\Phi_N|.\n\\]\nThe representation \\(u\\mapsto U_{u^d}\\), \\(d\\ne0\\), has the same invariants as \\(U\\), since the power map is onto.</p><p>Put \\(\\mathcal R=X\\times_YX\\). For \\(v_0=v-Pv\\) and \\(w_0=w-Pw\\), the pair error is \\(P(v_0U_{u^d}w_0)\\), and conditional independence gives\n\\[\n\\|P(v_0U_{u^d}w_0)\\|_2^2\n=\n\\int_{\\mathcal R}(v_0\\otimes\\bar v_0)\nU_{u^d}(w_0\\otimes\\bar w_0)\\,d\\mu_{\\mathcal R}.\n\\]\nThe second tensor has integral \\(\\int_Y|Pw_0|^2\\,d\\nu=0\\). Ergodicity and mean ergodicity prove the pair criterion.</p><p>The same criterion holds on \\(\\mathcal R\\) over \\(Y\\). For tensors,\n\\[\nP_{\\mathcal R}\\bigl((a_1\\otimes a_2)U_{u^d}(b_1\\otimes b_2)\\bigr)\n=P(a_1U_{u^d}b_1)P(a_2U_{u^d}b_2).\n\\]\nSubtracting the product of factor parts produces two bounded-factor multiples of pair errors. Finite sums of tensors follow by linearity. Conditional expectations onto finite rectangle-generated partitions approximate bounded functions in \\(L^2\\) with common sup bounds; contraction and invariance control the errors uniformly in \\(u\\).</p><p>On either \\(X\\) or \\(\\mathcal R\\), prove by induction that, for distinct nonzero \\(n_j\\), if one \\(Pv_{j_*}=0\\), then\n\\[\n\\left\\|\\operatorname{Avg}_{u\\in\\Phi_N}\n\\prod_{j=1}^lU_{u^{n_j}}v_j\\right\\|_2\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{*}\n\\]\nNormalize \\(\\|v_j\\|_\\infty\\le1\\). The case \\(l=1\\) is mean ergodicity. For the induction, set\n\\[\nV_u=\\prod_jU_{u^{n_j}}v_j,\\qquad\nF_{j,h}=U_{h^{n_j}}v_j\\,\\bar v_j.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\int V_{hu}\\bar V_u\\,d\\mu\n=\n\\int F_{l,h}\\prod_{j&lt;l}U_{u^{n_j-n_l}}F_{j,h}\\,d\\mu.\n\\]\nExpand each shifted factor into its projection and centered remainder. Induction kills all terms containing a centered shifted factor after averaging. In the remaining scalar integral replace the unshifted factor by its projection as well. Consequently\n\\[\n\\limsup_N\\left|\n\\operatorname{Avg}_{u\\in\\Phi_N}\\int V_{hu}\\bar V_u\\,d\\mu\n\\right|\n\\le a(h):=\\|PF_{j_*,h}\\|_2.\n\\]\nThis covers \\(j_*=l\\) by an \\(L^1\\), then \\(L^2\\), bound on the unshifted factor; otherwise use the same bound on its shifted factor.</p><p>The pair criterion gives vanishing averages of \\(a(h)^2\\), and hence of \\(a(h)\\), along every Følner sequence. For any fixed Følner sequence \\(\\Psi_M\\),\n\\[\n\\sup_{t\\in K}\\operatorname{Avg}_{h\\in\\Psi_M}a(ht)\\longrightarrow0,\n\\]\nbecause a subsequence of bad translates would itself be Følner.</p><p>For finite nonempty \\(S\\subset K\\), Følner invariance, Jensen, and expansion over shift pairs yield\n\\[\n\\limsup_N\\|\\operatorname{Avg}_{u\\in\\Phi_N}V_u\\|_2^2\n\\le\\frac1{|S|^2}\\sum_{h,h'\\in S}a(h(h')^{-1}).\n\\]\nTake \\(S=\\Psi_M\\) and then \\(M\\to\\infty\\). This proves (*).</p><p>Finally expand each \\(v_j=Pv_j+(v_j-Pv_j)\\). For a product \\(W_u=\\prod_jU_{u^{n_j}}w_j\\) containing a centered factor,\n\\[\n\\|PW_u\\|_2^2\n=\n\\int_{\\mathcal R}\\prod_j\nU_{u^{n_j}}(w_j\\otimes\\bar w_j)\\,d\\mu_{\\mathcal R}.\n\\]\nThe corresponding tensor is centered over \\(Y\\). Add a common integer to all exponents inside this scalar integral so none is zero, and apply (*) on \\(\\mathcal R\\). The average tends to zero. There are finitely many centered terms. The uncentered term is the product of projections, proving the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.7", "orig": "Lemma 3.3, Radial identities", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( w(|A-B|)\\le w(A)+w(B) \\qquad(A,B\\in F_{\\ge0}), \\) and, for every \\(r\\in F\\), \\(r>0\\), and every Følner sequence, \\( \\operatorname{Avg}_{u\\in\\Phi_N}m(2r|\\operatorname{Re}u|) \\longrightarrow m(r)^2. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mu\\) be an ergodic wild \\(K\\)-invariant probability on \\(D\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Apply Lemma 1.5 and let \\(Y\\) be its terminal factor.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  P=\\mathbb E(\\,\\cdot\\mid Y),\\qquad\n  g_z=P\\psi_z,\\qquad m(z)=\\|g_z\\|_2^2.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">By conditional independence, \\(m\\) is the Fourier transform of the difference law \\(\\nu_Y\\); by Lemma 1.4, that law is wild.</p><p class=\"li\">Covariance gives \\(g_{uz}=U_ug_z\\), so \\(m\\) is radial. Its radii lie in \\(F_{\\ge0}\\), and \\(m(r)\\) denotes its value at radius \\(r\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  w(r)=-\\log m(r)\\in[0,\\infty],\\qquad-\\log0=\\infty.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nw(|A-B|)\\le w(A)+w(B)\n\\qquad(A,B\\in F_{\\ge0}),\n\\]\nand, for every \\(r\\in F\\), \\(r&gt;0\\), and every Følner sequence,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Avg}_{u\\in\\Phi_N}m(2r|\\operatorname{Re}u|)\n\\longrightarrow m(r)^2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Here \\(0\\le m\\le1\\), \\(m(0)=1\\), \\(g_{-z}=\\bar g_z\\), and \\(\\|g_z\\|_\\infty\\le1\\).</p><p>For \\(A,B&gt;0\\), let \\(L=\\sqrt{AB}\\in F\\). For every \\(u\\in K\\),\n\\[\n|A-Bu^2|^2\n=(A-B)^2+4AB(\\operatorname{Im}u)^2\n=|-L+(A-B)u+Lu^2|^2.\n\\]\nFor the last equality, factor the expression as\n\\(u((A-B)+L(u-u^{-1}))\\).</p><p>Apply Lemma 1.6 first to\n\\[\n\\psi_{A-Bu^2}=\\psi_AU_{u^2}\\psi_{-B}.\n\\]\nIts projection is approximated in averaged squared \\(L^2\\)-error by \\(g_AU_{u^2}g_{-B}\\). The squared norms differ in averaged absolute value by a quantity tending to zero, since all norms are at most one. Mean ergodicity on \\(Y\\) gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Avg}_{u\\in\\Phi_N}m(A-Bu^2)\\longrightarrow m(A)m(B).\n\\]\nApply Lemma 1.6 to the other expression with exponents \\(0,1,2\\). Its projected character is approximated by\n\\[\ng_{-L}U_ug_{A-B}U_{u^2}g_L,\n\\]\nwhose squared norm is at most \\(m(|A-B|)\\). Equal moduli give equal \\(m\\)-values, so\n\\[\nm(A)m(B)\\le m(|A-B|).\n\\]\nTaking negative logarithms proves the difference inequality, including zero values with the stated convention. Cases \\(A=0\\) or \\(B=0\\) use \\(w(0)=0\\).</p><p>For the second identity, use \\(ru+r\\bar u=2r\\operatorname{Re}u\\) and exponents \\(1,-1\\). Lemma 1.6 reduces the average to squared norms of \\(U_ug_rU_{u^{-1}}g_r\\), equal to\n\\[\n\\int_Y|g_r|^2U_{u^{-2}}|g_r|^2\\,d\\nu.\n\\]\nMean ergodicity and surjectivity of \\(u\\mapsto u^{-2}\\) give \\(m(r)^2\\). Radiality supplies the absolute value.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.8", "orig": "Lemma 3.4, General position in a number field", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are arbitrarily large finite sets \\(S\\subset K\\cap N\\) such that every \\(d_0\\) distinct elements of \\(S\\) form a \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-basis of \\(N\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(N_0\\subset F\\) be a number field.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(N=N_0(i)\\) and \\(d_0=[N:\\mathbb Q]\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are arbitrarily large finite sets \\(S\\subset K\\cap N\\) such that every \\(d_0\\) distinct elements of \\(S\\) form a \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-basis of \\(N\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(t\\in N_0\\), put\n\\[\nu(t)=\\frac{1+it}{1-it}\\in K\\cap N.\n\\]\nIn rational coordinates from a \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-basis of \\(N_0\\), its coordinates in \\(N\\) are rational functions over \\(\\mathbb Q\\), obtained by inverting multiplication by \\(1-it\\). The denominator is nonzero for real \\(t\\).</p><p>No nonzero \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-linear functional \\(\\ell:N\\to\\mathbb Q\\) vanishes on every \\(u(t)\\). Otherwise, for fixed \\(a\\in N_0\\), \\(\\ell(u(ha))\\) is a rational function vanishing at every rational \\(h\\), hence identically zero. The formal expansion\n\\[\nu(ha)=1+2iha-2h^2a^2+\\cdots\n\\]\ngives \\(\\ell(1)=\\ell(ia)=\\ell(a^2)=0\\). Squares span \\(N_0\\), since\n\\[\n4a=(a+1)^2-(a-1)^2.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\ell\\) vanishes on \\(N_0+iN_0=N\\), a contradiction.</p><p>For each proper rational subspace, choose an annihilating nonzero rational functional. Its value on \\(u(t)\\) has nonzero numerator polynomial. A finite union of proper subspaces cannot contain all \\(u(t)\\), because a product of these nonzero polynomials cannot vanish on all rational affine space. Choose each new element outside the spans of every subset of at most \\(d_0-1\\) earlier elements. This gives the required sets.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.9", "orig": "Lemma 3.5, Addition under a cap", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( w(z)\\le4MH. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(w:F_{\\ge0}\\to[0,\\infty]\\) satisfy \\(w(0)=0\\) and\n  \\[\n  w(|A-B|)\\le w(A)+w(B).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p_1,\\ldots,p_q\\in F_{\\ge0}\\),\n  \\[\n  p=\\max_jp_j&gt;0,\\qquad w(p_j)\\le H&lt;\\infty.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose\n  \\[\n  z=\\sum_{j=1}^qa_jp_j\\in[0,p),\n  \\qquad a_j\\in\\mathbb Z,\\qquad\\sum_j|a_j|\\le M.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nw(z)\\le4MH.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Keep partial sums as residues in \\([0,p)\\). Positive inputs below \\(p\\) have cost at most \\(H\\), and input \\(p\\) has zero residue. A nonzero residue \\(b\\) has inverse \\(p-b\\), of cost at most \\(w(p)+w(b)\\). Thus every signed input has residue cost at most \\(2H\\).</p><p>For residues \\(a,b\\), if \\(a+b&lt;p\\), then\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nw(a+b)\n&amp;=w\\bigl(p-((p-a)-b)\\bigr)\\\\\n&amp;\\le w(p)+w((p-a)-b)\\\\\n&amp;\\le w(p)+w(p-a)+w(b)\\\\\n&amp;\\le2w(p)+w(a)+w(b).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nIf \\(a+b&gt;p\\), its residue is \\(a-(p-b)\\), of cost at most \\(w(a)+w(p)+w(b)\\). Equality gives zero residue. Each signed input increases cost by at most \\(4H\\). Expand the combination into at most \\(M\\) inputs. Its final residue is \\(z\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Proposition 4.2, From proper to weak measurable colorings", "depth": 0, "snippet": "If the plane admits an unrestricted proper \\(k\\)-coloring, then it admits a weak measurable \\(k\\)-coloring.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\) be a positive integer.</p><p class=\"li\">Use proper colorings and weak measurable colorings as defined in Theorems 1 and 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Use \\(F,E,K,D,C,j\\) from Proposition 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If the plane admits an unrestricted proper \\(k\\)-coloring, then it admits a weak measurable \\(k\\)-coloring.</p>", "proof": "<p>Restrict the given coloring to \\(E\\). Let \\(\\Omega\\subset\\{1,\\ldots,k\\}^E\\) be the nonempty closed space of proper labelings of the unit-distance graph on \\(E\\). The countable group \\(E\\rtimes K\\) acts continuously and preserves the constraints. It is amenable as an extension of abelian groups. Averaging a point mass along a finite-set Følner sequence and taking a weakly convergent subsequence yields an invariant Borel probability \\(\\mu\\).</p><p>Let \\(f_i\\) indicate label \\(i\\) at the origin, with \\(T_zf_i\\) indicating label \\(i\\) at \\(z\\). Rotations fix \\(f_i\\), and\n\\[\n\\int_\\Omega f_iT_uf_i\\,d\\mu=0\n\\qquad(u\\in K).\n\\]\nBy Lemma 2.1, write\n\\[\np_i=\\mathbb E(f_i\\mid\\mathcal F_c),\\qquad b_i=f_i-p_i.\n\\]\nThen \\(0\\le p_i\\le1\\), \\(\\sum_ip_i=1\\), and \\(p_i,b_i\\) are rotation-fixed.</p><p>The spectral measure of \\(b_i\\) is \\(K\\)-invariant by covariance and Fourier uniqueness, and gives \\(C\\) zero mass. Proposition 1 makes it a multiple of Haar, including the zero multiple. Hence\n\\[\n\\langle b_i,T_zb_i\\rangle=0\\qquad(z\\ne0).\n\\]\nTranslation invariance of the two spectral subspaces kills the cross terms, giving\n\\[\n\\int p_iT_up_i\\,d\\mu=0\\qquad(u\\in K).\n\\]\nDensity of algebraic directions and strong continuity extend this to all \\(u\\in S^1\\). Therefore, for every \\(x\\in\\mathbb R^2\\),\n\\[\n\\int(T_xp_i)(T_{x+u}p_i)\\,d\\mu\n=\\langle p_i,T_up_i\\rangle=0.\n\\tag{*}\n\\]</p><p>The continuous \\(L^2(\\mu)\\)-valued map \\(x\\mapsto T_xp_i\\) has a jointly measurable representative \\(H_i(x,\\omega)\\) correct for almost every \\(x\\). On each bounded spatial domain, use finite-valued simple approximations with summable integrated squared errors, choose measurable representatives of their finitely many values, and take a product-\\(L^2\\) limit. A subsequence converges in \\(L^2(\\mu)\\) for almost every \\(x\\). A countable exhaustion gives the field globally. Borel versions can be used; taking real parts and clamping to \\([0,1]\\) preserves the representation.</p><p>Let \\(N\\) be the plane-null exceptional set of positions. Fubini and translation invariance give\n\\[\n\\int\\!\\int_{S^1}(1_N(x)+1_N(x+u))\\,d\\sigma(u)\\,dx=0\n\\]\non every bounded domain. Thus (*) applies to these representatives for almost every \\((x,u)\\). Tonelli gives, for every positive integer \\(m\\),\n\\[\n\\int_\\Omega\\sum_i\\int_{B(0,m)}\\int_{S^1}\nH_i(x,\\omega)H_i(x+u,\\omega)\\,d\\sigma(u)\\,dx\\,d\\mu(\\omega)=0.\n\\]\nChoose one sample satisfying all these countably many conditions and\n\\(\\sum_iH_i(x,\\omega)=1\\) for almost every \\(x\\). At those points choose the least index with positive field; elsewhere assign any label. This is measurable. A same-color pair outside the spatial exceptional set has a positive corresponding product, which occurs only on a product-null set. Pairs hitting the exceptional set are also product-null. The resulting coloring is weak measurable.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.1, Continuous vectors and positivity", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The subspace \\(\\mathcal H_c\\) is \\(L^2\\) of a factor and is the spectral subspace on \\(C\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let a standard probability space carry a measure-preserving action of the additive discrete group \\(E\\), with Koopman operators \\(T_z\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In the limit below, \\(E\\) has its usual subspace topology in \\(\\mathbb R^2\\):\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal H_c=\n  \\left\\{v\\in L^2:\\lim_{\\substack{z\\to0\\\\z\\in E}}\\|T_zv-v\\|_2=0\\right\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The subspace \\(\\mathcal H_c\\) is \\(L^2\\) of a factor and is the spectral subspace on \\(C\\). Its operators extend to a strongly continuous unitary representation \\(T_x\\), \\(x\\in\\mathbb R^2\\), preserving positivity and the constant one.</p><p>If rotations \\(V_u\\), \\(u\\in K\\), satisfy\n\\[\nV_uT_zV_u^{-1}=T_{uz},\n\\]\nthen \\(\\mathcal H_c\\) and its orthogonal projection are rotation invariant.</p>", "proof": "<p>The subspace is closed, contains constants, and is translation invariant. Closedness follows from\n\\[\n\\|T_zv-v\\|_2\\le2\\|v-w\\|_2+\\|T_zw-w\\|_2.\n\\]</p><p>For the scalar spectral measure \\(\\lambda_v\\) on \\(D\\),\n\\[\n\\langle v,T_zv\\rangle=\\int_D\\psi_z\\,d\\lambda_v,\\qquad\n\\|T_zv-v\\|_2^2=\\int_D|\\psi_z-1|^2\\,d\\lambda_v.\n\\]\nSupport on \\(C\\) gives continuity by dominated convergence. Conversely, for \\(v\\in\\mathcal H_c\\), its coefficient is uniformly continuous on \\(E\\), since\n\\[\n|\\langle v,T_{z+h}v\\rangle-\\langle v,T_zv\\rangle|\n\\le\\|v\\|_2\\|T_hv-v\\|_2.\n\\]\nExtend it to a continuous positive-definite function on \\(\\mathbb R^2\\). Bochner's theorem and pushforward under \\(j\\), followed by Fourier uniqueness on \\(D\\), show \\(\\lambda_v(C^c)=0\\).</p><p>The Koopman property gives closure under conjugation and, for bounded continuous vectors, products:\n\\[\n\\|T_z(vw)-vw\\|_2\n\\le\\|v\\|_\\infty\\|T_zw-w\\|_2+\n\\|w\\|_\\infty\\|T_zv-v\\|_2.\n\\]\nLipschitz composition also preserves \\(\\mathcal H_c\\). In particular, bounded radial truncations remain in it and converge in \\(L^2\\).</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal F_c\\) be generated by its bounded members. They form a bounded unital star-algebra. Stone–Weierstrass on finite-coordinate compact ranges, density of continuous functions in their \\(L^2\\) distributions, and the monotone-class theorem show that their \\(L^2\\)-closure is \\(L^2(\\mathcal F_c)\\). Closedness and truncation give\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_c=L^2(\\mathcal F_c).\n\\]\nThe projection is conditional expectation.</p><p>For \\(x\\in\\mathbb R^2\\), choose \\(z_n\\in E\\), \\(z_n\\to x\\), and define \\(T_xv=\\lim_nT_{z_n}v\\). The Cauchy property follows from\n\\[\n\\|T_{z_n}v-T_{z_m}v\\|_2\n=\\|T_{z_n-z_m}v-v\\|_2.\n\\]\nIndependence of approximation, the group law, inverses, unitarity, and strong continuity follow by strong limits. The positive cone is closed, so positivity passes to the limit; \\(T_x1=1\\).</p><p>Finally covariance gives\n\\[\n\\|T_zV_uv-V_uv\\|_2\n=\\|T_{u^{-1}z}v-v\\|_2\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nRotations and their inverses preserve the subspace, so commute with its projection.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every finite plane configuration has a properly \\(k\\)-colored translate among density-one points of the color classes.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\) be a positive integer.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(c:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\{1,\\ldots,k\\}\\) be a weak measurable coloring with classes \\(A_i\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every finite plane configuration has a properly \\(k\\)-colored translate among density-one points of the color classes. Consequently the entire plane admits an unrestricted proper \\(k\\)-coloring.</p>", "proof": "<p>By Lemma 3.1, the conull union of density-point classes is properly colored. Every finite configuration has a translate inside it: allowable translation vectors form a finite intersection of conull sets.</p><p>In the compact product \\(\\{1,\\ldots,k\\}^{\\mathbb R^2}\\), each unit-edge condition is closed and depends on two coordinates. Every finite family involves a finite configuration and is satisfiable. Product compactness in ZFC gives a coloring satisfying all conditions. This is the de Bruijn–Erdős graph compactness principle.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.3, Density-point properness", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The full-density-point set of each \\(A_i\\) contains no unit pair.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the weak coloring of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">A full-density point of \\(A_i\\) is a point \\(x\\) with\n  \\[\n  \\lim_{h\\downarrow0}\\frac{|A_i\\cap B(x,h)|}{\\pi h^2}=1.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The full-density-point set of each \\(A_i\\) contains no unit pair. These sets are disjoint and their union is conull.</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose \\(x,y=x+u\\), \\(|u|=1\\), both have density one of \\(A_i\\). Choose \\(x'\\) uniformly in \\(B(x,h)\\), and independently choose \\(u'\\) uniformly on a positive-measure arc satisfying \\(|u'-u|&lt;h\\). Then\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(x'\\notin A_i)=o(1).\n\\]\nThe distribution of \\(x'+u'\\) is a mixture of translated uniform disks, with density at most \\(1/(\\pi h^2)\\) and support in \\(B(y,2h)\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(x'+u'\\notin A_i)\n\\le\\frac{|B(y,2h)\\setminus A_i|}{\\pi h^2}=o(1).\n\\]\nFor small \\(h\\), both endpoints belong to \\(A_i\\) with positive probability. Their parameter distribution is absolutely continuous with respect to \\(dx\\,d\\sigma(u)\\), contradicting the weak condition.</p><p>Lebesgue differentiation makes the union conull. Disjoint classes cannot both have density one at the same point.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The trace families are nonempty and compact, consist of nonnegative sum-one vectors, and satisfy \\( x_n\\to x,\\quad b^n\\in\\mathcal W_{x_n},\\quad b^n\\rightharpoonup^*b \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad b\\in\\mathcal W_x. \\) At …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For this proposition and the subsequent geometric propositions, assume a weak measurable five-coloring with Borel classes \\(A_i\\) forming a partition, and put \\(a_i=1_{A_i}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A point is typical if it has density one of its actual color. Typical points form a conull set, and unit pairs of typical points have different colors by Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Define the closed spatial essential supports\n  \\[\n  H_i=\\{q:|A_i\\cap B(q,r)|&gt;0\\text{ for every }r&gt;0\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(S=S^1\\), let \\(R\\) rotate by \\(\\kappa=\\pi/3\\), and let \\(J\\) rotate by \\(\\pi/2\\). Directions are parallel if equal or opposite.</p><p class=\"li\">A typical circle sampling \\((y,r)\\), \\(r&gt;0\\), has \\(y+re\\) typical for almost every \\(e\\in S\\).</p><p class=\"li\">As in original Definition 5.1, let \\(\\mathcal W_x\\) consist of weak-* limits\n  \\[\n  b=\\mathrm{w}^*\\!-\\!\\lim_n(a_i(y_n+r_ne))_{i=1}^5,\n  \\qquad y_n\\to x,\\quad r_n\\to1,\n  \\]\n  with all samplings typical. Constant sequences are allowed.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  D_{x,i}=\\operatorname*{ess\\,union}_{b\\in\\mathcal W_x}\\{e:b_i(e)&gt;0\\},\n  \\qquad P_x(e)=\\{i:e\\in D_{x,i}\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The trace families are nonempty and compact, consist of nonnegative sum-one vectors, and satisfy\n\\[\nx_n\\to x,\\quad b^n\\in\\mathcal W_{x_n},\\quad b^n\\rightharpoonup^*b\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad b\\in\\mathcal W_x.\n\\]\nAt every fixed center:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. almost every palette is nonempty;</p><p class=\"li num\">2. for almost every \\((e,v)\\in S\\times S\\),\n   \\[\n   c(x+e+v)\\notin P_x(e)\\cup P_x(v);\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. if \\(e\\) is a density-one point of \\(D_{x,i}\\), then \\(A_i\\) has spatial density zero at \\(x+e+v\\) for every nonparallel \\(v\\);</p><p class=\"li num\">4. under a smooth representation\n   \\[\n   z+f=x+s(f)+t(f)\n   \\]\n   on open arcs, where \\(s,t\\in S\\) are nonparallel and \\(s\\) is a local angular diffeomorphism,\n   \\[\n   P_z(f)\\cap P_x(s(f))=\\varnothing\n   \\quad\\text{almost everywhere};\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">5. \\(P_x(f)\\cap P_x(Rf)=\\varnothing\\) almost everywhere.</p><p>Exceptional angular sets may depend on the fixed centers and maps. No joint measurability in \\(x\\) is asserted.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 4.1 establishes the trace and palette facts. Lemma 4.2 establishes the three transport assertions. Lemma 4.3 gives sixty-degree exclusion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "compactness and countability facts following Definition 5.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The trace families have the nonemptiness, compactness, closed-graph, and sum-one properties stated in Proposition 4.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the trace and palette definitions in Proposition 4.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The trace families have the nonemptiness, compactness, closed-graph, and sum-one properties stated in Proposition 4. Each essential union \\(D_{x,i}\\) is generated by a countable trace subfamily. Every individual trace has its positive coordinates in \\(P_x\\) almost everywhere, and \\(P_x\\) is nonempty almost everywhere.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fubini shows that for fixed \\(r\\), almost every center gives a typical sampling; for fixed center, almost every positive radius does so. Choose typical radius-one samplings with centers approaching \\(x\\). The bounded ball of \\(L^\\infty(S)\\) is weak-* compact and metrizable because \\(L^1(S)\\) is separable. Subsequence limits exist. Nonnegativity and sum one pass to weak-* limits by nonnegative tests.</p><p>For closed graph, fix a bounded-ball weak-* metric. From a sequence realizing \\(b^n\\), choose a sampling within \\(1/n\\) of \\(b^n\\), with center within \\(1/n\\) of \\(x_n\\) and radius within \\(1/n\\) of one. This diagonal sequence realizes \\(b\\). In particular \\(\\mathcal W_x\\) is compact.</p><p>For a fixed label, let \\(M_i\\) be the supremum of measures of finite unions of positivity sets. Choose finite unions approaching \\(M_i\\), and take their countable union. Its measure is \\(M_i\\); any positivity set with positive measure outside would contradict the supremum after adjoining it to a sufficiently large finite partial union. This gives the essential union and the support assertion. Any one sum-one trace proves palette nonemptiness.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "Lemma 5.2, Palette transfer", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assertions 2–4 of Proposition 4 hold, with exceptional sets allowed to depend on the fixed centers and specified maps.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 4.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Assertions 2–4 of Proposition 4 hold, with exceptional sets allowed to depend on the fixed centers and specified maps.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(b\\in\\mathcal W_x\\), realized by \\((y_n,r_n)\\). The maps\n\\[\nG_n(e,v)=y_n+r_ne+v\n\\]\nhave angular Jacobian \\(r_n|\\sin(\\arg v-\\arg e)|\\), nonsingular off a product-null set. Change of variables on countably many smaller patches shows that the second point is typical almost everywhere. Typical-point properness gives\n\\[\na_i(y_n+r_ne)a_i(y_n+r_ne+v)=0\n\\quad\\text{for almost every }(e,v).\n\\]</p><p>Furthermore,\n\\[\na_i(y_n+r_ne+v)\\longrightarrow a_i(x+e+v)\n\\quad\\text{in }L^1(S\\times S).\n\\]\nTo see this, discard \\(|\\sin(\\arg v-\\arg e)|\\le\\delta\\). On the remainder, Jacobians are uniformly bounded below and multiplicity is at most two, by intersection of two circles. The coincident-circle case is antiparallel and discarded. Pushforward densities are uniformly bounded on a fixed compact region. Approximate \\(a_i\\) there in plane \\(L^1\\) by a continuous \\([0,1]\\)-valued function. Density bounds control composition errors; the continuous compositions converge uniformly. Then let \\(\\delta\\downarrow0\\).</p><p>Replace the second factor strongly and the first weak-*. This yields\n\\[\n\\int_{S\\times S}b_i(e)a_i(x+e+v)\\,d\\sigma(e)d\\sigma(v)=0.\n\\]\nNonnegativity, countable generating trace families, and interchange of \\(e,v\\) prove assertion 2.</p><p>For assertion 3, the sum map is a local diffeomorphism near any nonparallel pair \\((e,v)\\). The inverse image of \\(B(x+e+v,h)\\) lies in angular intervals of length \\(O(h)\\), with bounded change-of-variables factors. Assertion 2 implies\n\\[\n|A_i\\cap B(x+e+v,h)|\n\\le Ch\\,|[e-Ch,e+Ch]\\setminus D_{x,i}|=o(h^2).\n\\]</p><p>For assertion 4, work on a smaller compact arc with one inverse branch. A trace at \\(z\\), realized by \\((z_n,r_n)\\), has perturbed representations\n\\[\nz_n+r_nf=x+s_n(f)+t_n(f),\n\\]\nwith \\(s_n,t_n\\to s,t\\) in \\(C^1\\), remaining nonparallel, and uniformly bounded inverse derivatives for \\(s_n\\). Continuous approximation and change of variables give\n\\[\n1_{D_{x,i}}(s_n(f))\\longrightarrow1_{D_{x,i}}(s(f))\n\\quad\\text{in }L^1.\n\\]\nAlmost every \\(f\\) with \\(s_n(f)\\in D_{x,i}\\) maps to a density-one direction. Assertion 3 gives density zero of \\(A_i\\) at \\(z_n+r_nf\\). Typical sampling therefore implies\n\\[\na_i(z_n+r_nf)1_{D_{x,i}}(s_n(f))=0\n\\]\nalmost everywhere. Pass strongly in the second factor and weak-* in the first. Countable generating traces at \\(z\\), the five labels, and a countable arc cover prove assertion 4.</p><p>The second map \\(t\\) may be constant: only \\(s\\) must control angular null-set preimages. In particular \\(z=x+a\\), \\(s(f)=f\\), \\(t(f)=a\\) is admissible off \\(f=\\pm a\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "Corollary 5.4, Sixty-degree exclusion", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every fixed \\(x\\), \\( P_x(f)\\cap P_x(Rf)=\\varnothing \\quad\\text{for almost every }f\\in S. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 4.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every fixed \\(x\\),\n\\[\nP_x(f)\\cap P_x(Rf)=\\varnothing\n\\quad\\text{for almost every }f\\in S.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Use\n\\[\nx+f=x+Rf+R^{-1}f\n\\]\nin Lemma 4.2. The endpoint directions are nonparallel and the first endpoint map is a rotation.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Lemma 5.5, Binary-center separation", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is an absolute \\(\\delta_*>0\\) such that any two distinct centers binary for the same fixed pair have distance at least \\(\\delta_*\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Assume the weak measurable five-coloring and palettes of Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix distinct labels \\(i_+,i_-\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A center \\(x\\) is binary for this pair if\n  \\[\n  P_x(e)\\cap\\{i_+,i_-\\}\\ne\\varnothing\n  \\quad\\text{for almost every }e.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Sixty-degree exclusion makes this intersection a singleton almost everywhere. Encode its unique selection by a measurable \\(2\\pi\\)-periodic function \\(s_x:\\mathbb R\\to\\{-1,1\\}\\). Then\n  \\[\n  s_x(t+\\kappa)=-s_x(t)\n  \\quad\\text{almost everywhere},\n  \\]\n  so each sign has measure \\(\\pi\\) in a period.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an absolute \\(\\delta_*&gt;0\\) such that any two distinct centers binary for the same fixed pair have distance at least \\(\\delta_*\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>If two binary centers had \\(0&lt;d&lt;\\delta_*\\), Lemma 5.1 would make\n\\[\nA=\\{t:s_x(t)=1\\}\n\\]\ninvariant under the six maps. It has measure \\(\\pi\\) in each period. Lemma 5.2 makes it conull, a contradiction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "These are smooth degree-one circle diffeomorphism lifts for small \\(d\\), and \\(s_x\\circ F_j=s_x\\) almost everywhere.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(x,y\\) be distinct binary centers for the same pair.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(d=|y-x|\\), and rotate angular coordinates so \\(y-x=d&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For sufficiently small \\(d\\), define\n  \\[\n  \\Theta_d(t)=t+\\operatorname{Im}\\log(1+de^{-it}),\\qquad\n  \\alpha_\\pm(t)=\\Theta_d(t)\\pm\\arccos(|d+e^{it}|/2).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  H=\\alpha_+\\circ\\alpha_-^{-1},\\quad\n  F(t)=H(t)-2\\kappa,\\quad\n  F_j(t)=j\\kappa+F(t-j\\kappa),\\quad0\\le j\\le5.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>These are smooth degree-one circle diffeomorphism lifts for small \\(d\\), and \\(s_x\\circ F_j=s_x\\) almost everywhere. There are absolute \\(c_0,C_0,d_1&gt;0\\), \\(d_1&lt;1/2\\), such that, for \\(0&lt;d&lt;d_1\\),\n\\[\n\\max_j(F_j(t)-t)\\ge c_0d,\\qquad\n\\max_jF'_j(t)\\ge1+c_0d,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n|F_j(t)-t|,\\quad |F'_j(t)-1|,\\quad |F''_j(t)|\\le C_0d.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The formulas give\n\\[\ny+e^{it}=x+e^{i\\alpha_+(t)}+e^{i\\alpha_-(t)}.\n\\]\nFor \\(d&lt;1/2\\), the sum has length strictly between zero and two. Its two directions are nonparallel, and \\(\\alpha'_\\pm\\to1\\) uniformly. Palette transport gives\n\\[\ns_x(\\alpha_+(t))=s_x(\\alpha_-(t))=-s_y(t)\n\\]\nalmost everywhere. Thus \\(s_x\\) is \\(H\\)-invariant. It is also invariant under \\(2\\kappa\\), so is \\(F\\)-invariant. The flip identity under \\(j\\kappa\\) gives invariance under every \\(F_j\\).</p><p>Uniform Taylor expansions in \\(C^2\\) give\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Im}\\log(1+de^{-it})=-d\\sin t+O_{C^2}(d^2),\n\\]\n\\[\n|d+e^{it}|=1+d\\cos t+O_{C^2}(d^2),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\alpha_\\pm(t)=t\\pm\\kappa-d\\sin t\n\\mp\\frac d{\\sqrt3}\\cos t+O_{C^2}(d^2).\n\\]\nWriting \\(a_\\pm(t)=-\\sin t\\mp\\cos t/\\sqrt3\\), composition with the inverse gives\n\\[\nF(t)=t-\\frac{2d}{\\sqrt3}\\cos(t+\\kappa)+O_{C^2}(d^2)\n=t+\\frac{2d}{\\sqrt3}\\sin(t-\\pi/6)+O_{C^2}(d^2).\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nF_j(t)=t+\\frac{2d}{\\sqrt3}\\sin(t-j\\kappa-\\pi/6)+O_{C^2}(d^2).\n\\]\nAmong six phases spaced by \\(\\pi/3\\), the maximum sine and maximum cosine are at least \\(\\sqrt3/2\\). Absorb the quadratic remainders to obtain the stated estimates.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The set \\(A\\) has full measure in a period.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the maps and constants of Lemma 5.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose\n  \\[\n  0&lt;L\\le\\min\\{c_0/C_0,\\pi/4\\},\\qquad\n  \\delta_*=\\min\\{d_1,(2C_0)^{-1},L/(2C_0)\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(0&lt;d&lt;\\delta_*\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A\\subset\\mathbb R\\) be measurable, \\(2\\pi\\)-periodic, of positive measure in a period, and invariant modulo null sets under every \\(F_j\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The set \\(A\\) has full measure in a period.</p>", "proof": "<p>Here \\(1/2\\le F'_j\\le3/2\\). Maps and inverses preserve null sets. All finite compositions preserve \\(A\\) modulo null sets; only countably many words occur, so adaptive choices cause no exceptional-set problem.</p><p>Start with an interval \\(I_0\\) of length \\(\\ell_0&lt;L\\). For \\(\\ell_r&lt;L\\), choose \\(F_{j_r}\\) whose derivative at the midpoint is at least \\(1+c_0d\\). The second-derivative bound gives\n\\[\nF'_{j_r}(t)\\ge1+\\tfrac12c_0d=:\\lambda&gt;1\n\\quad(t\\in I_r).\n\\]\nSet \\(I_{r+1}=F_{j_r}(I_r)\\). Stop at the first \\(N\\) with \\(\\ell_N\\ge L\\); then\n\\[\nL\\le\\ell_N\\le(1+C_0d)L.\n\\]\nFor \\(G=F_{j_{N-1}}\\circ\\cdots\\circ F_{j_0}\\),\n\\[\n|(\\log F'_j)'|\\le2C_0d,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\n\\operatorname{osc}_{I_0}\\log G'\n\\le2C_0d\\sum_{r&lt;N}\\ell_r\n\\le\\frac{4C_0(1+C_0d)L}{c_0}\n\\le\\frac{6C_0L}{c_0}.\n\\]\nThus the distortion ratio is bounded by\n\\[\nD_{\\rm dist}=\\exp(6C_0L/c_0),\n\\]\nindependently of \\(I_0,N,d\\). Invariance and change of variables give\n\\[\n\\frac{|I_N\\setminus A|}{|I_N|}\n\\le D_{\\rm dist}\\frac{|I_0\\setminus A|}{|I_0|}.\n\\]</p><p>Apply this to intervals shrinking to a density point of \\(A\\). The resulting intervals \\(J_n\\) have lengths in \\([L,(1+C_0d)L]\\) and \\(|J_n\\setminus A|\\to0\\). Translate by periods, pass to convergent endpoints, and obtain an interval \\([a,b]\\) of length at least \\(L\\) on which \\(A\\) is conull.</p><p>Choose \\(j\\) with \\(F_j(b)\\ge b+c_0d\\). Its image interval is conull in \\(A\\), and\n\\[\nF_j(a)\\le a+C_0d&lt;a+L\\le b.\n\\]\nIt overlaps \\([a,b]\\), extending the conull interval to \\([a,F_j(b)]\\). Repeat. After finitely many steps its length is at least \\(2\\pi\\), so periodicity gives full measure.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 5.6, Palette cardinality", "depth": 0, "snippet": "At every fixed center \\(x\\), \\( 1\\le|P_x(e)|\\le2 \\quad\\text{for almost every }e\\in S. \\) The exceptional angular set may depend on \\(x\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Assume the weak measurable five-coloring and palettes of Proposition 4.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At every fixed center \\(x\\),\n\\[\n1\\le|P_x(e)|\\le2\n\\quad\\text{for almost every }e\\in S.\n\\]\nThe exceptional angular set may depend on \\(x\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Nonemptiness follows from a sum-one trace. If the upper bound fails, some fixed triple \\(A\\) of labels belongs to \\(P_x(e)\\) on a positive-measure angular set \\(B\\).</p><p>Palette exclusion and Fubini imply that for almost every \\(e\\in B\\), the actual circle \\(v\\mapsto x+e+v\\) uses only the complementary pair almost everywhere. Almost every such circle is typical, because the two-angle sum map pulls the plane-null atypical set back to a product-null set away from parallel directions.</p><p>The actual radius-one sample at \\(x+e\\) is therefore a trace, using a constant sequence, supported on that pair. Its maximal palette intersects the pair almost everywhere; sixty-degree exclusion makes the selection unique and alternating. Thus \\(x+e\\) is binary for the same fixed pair.</p><p>A positive-measure set of good angles gives infinitely many such centers on the compact circle \\(x+S\\). Proposition 5 makes them uniformly separated. A uniformly separated subset of a compact metric space is finite, a contradiction.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Lemma 6.2, An edge has an avoiding trace", "depth": 0, "snippet": "If \\(ij\\in\\Gamma_x\\), there is \\(b^{ij}\\in\\mathcal W_x\\) with \\( b_i^{ij}=b_j^{ij}=0 \\quad\\text{almost everywhere}. \\) Consequently, \\( P_x(e)\\not\\subset\\{i,j\\} \\quad\\text{for almost every }e. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Assume the weak measurable five-coloring of Proposition 4, with label set \\(\\mathcal I\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For distinct labels \\(i,j\\), bounded ball \\(B\\), and \\(h\\in\\mathbb R^2\\), define\n  \\[\n  D_{ij}(B,h)=\n  \\{q\\in B:(c(q),c(q+h))\\in\\{(i,j),(j,i)\\}\\}.\n  \\]\n  The second endpoint need not belong to \\(B\\).</p><p class=\"li\">As in original Definition 6.1, define the graph \\(\\Gamma_x\\) on \\(\\mathcal I\\) by\n  \\[\n  ij\\in\\Gamma_x\n  \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n  \\limsup_{\\substack{h\\to0\\\\h\\ne0}}\n  \\frac{|D_{ij}(B,h)|}{|h|}&gt;0\n  \\]\n  for every positive finite-radius ball centered at \\(x\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If \\(ij\\in\\Gamma_x\\), there is \\(b^{ij}\\in\\mathcal W_x\\) with\n\\[\nb_i^{ij}=b_j^{ij}=0\n\\quad\\text{almost everywhere}.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\nP_x(e)\\not\\subset\\{i,j\\}\n\\quad\\text{for almost every }e.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For fixed \\(h\\), almost every root has \\(q,q+h\\) typical and both centered unit-circle samplings typical. At a transition \\(s\\to t\\), the first circle avoids \\(s\\), the second avoids \\(t\\), and the first-circle mass of \\(t\\) is\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(a_t-a_t(\\cdot+h))(q).\n\\]\nThus at an \\(i,j\\) transition, the first-circle mass of the two colors is bounded by\n\\[\n|\\mathcal S(a_i-a_i(\\cdot+h))(q)|\n+\n|\\mathcal S(a_j-a_j(\\cdot+h))(q)|.\n\\]</p><p>For a ball \\(B\\) about \\(x\\), edge presence gives arbitrarily small \\(h\\ne0\\) with transition area at least \\(c_B|h|\\), for some \\(c_B&gt;0\\). Cauchy–Schwarz and Lemma 7.2 bound the mean displayed mass on this transition set by\n\\[\no(\\sqrt{|h|})/\\sqrt{c_B|h|}=o(1).\n\\]\nTake balls \\(B_m\\) shrinking to \\(x\\). After fixing each \\(B_m,c_{B_m}\\), choose \\(h_m\\) sufficiently small and a good transition root \\(q_m\\in B_m\\) with first-circle mass below \\(1/m\\). A weak-* subsequential trace at \\(x\\) avoids both labels. Its sum-one and palette-support properties yield the final assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "observations after Definition 6.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For each pair \\(ij\\), its edge-presence locus is closed, and \\( ij\\in\\Gamma_x\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x\\in H_i\\cap H_j. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the transition graphs of Proposition 7.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For each pair \\(ij\\), its edge-presence locus is closed, and\n\\[\nij\\in\\Gamma_x\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x\\in H_i\\cap H_j.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>A ball about a limit point contains a smaller ball about a nearby edge-presence point; transition sets are monotone in the ball. This proves closedness.</p><p>In any neighborhood of \\(x\\), use a smaller ball and sufficiently small \\(h\\) so both endpoints remain in the neighborhood. A positive-area transition set gives positive area of each endpoint color there; translation preserves area.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "estimate (6.3) in the proof of Lemma 6.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\|\\mathcal S(a_s-a_s(\\cdot+h))\\|_{L^2(B)} =o(\\sqrt{|h|}) \\qquad(h\\to0) \\) with \\(h\\) approaching zero in arbitrary directions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Define circle averaging by\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal Sg(q)=\\int_Sg(q+e)\\,d\\sigma(e).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(B\\) be a fixed bounded ball and \\(a_s\\) a color indicator.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\|\\mathcal S(a_s-a_s(\\cdot+h))\\|_{L^2(B)}\n=o(\\sqrt{|h|})\n\\qquad(h\\to0)\n\\]\nwith \\(h\\) approaching zero in arbitrary directions.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose boundedly supported \\(g\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^2)\\) agreeing with \\(a_s\\) on a ball containing \\(B+S+h\\) and \\(B+S\\) for all small \\(h\\).</p><p>The Fourier transform of normalized circle measure satisfies\n\\[\n|\\widehat\\sigma(\\xi)|\\le C(1+|\\xi|)^{-1/2}.\n\\]\nAfter rotation, the integral is a constant multiple of \\(\\int_0^{2\\pi}e^{ir\\cos t}\\,dt\\). For \\(r\\ge1\\), removing \\(|\\sin t|\\le r^{-1/2}\\) costs \\(O(r^{-1/2})\\). On each remaining interval, integration by parts with derivative \\(-ir\\sin t\\) gives endpoint and integral bounds \\(O(r^{-1/2})\\). Small \\(r\\) is immediate.</p><p>By Plancherel, the squared global norm divided by \\(|h|\\) is bounded by the integral of \\(|\\widehat g(\\xi)|^2\\) times\n\\[\n\\frac C{1+|\\xi|}\n\\frac{\\min\\{C|h|^2|\\xi|^2,4\\}}{|h|}.\n\\]\nThis multiplier is uniformly bounded by splitting at \\(|\\xi|=|h|^{-1}\\), and tends pointwise to zero. Dominated convergence proves the estimate.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Lemma 6.3, Strict state separation", "depth": 0, "snippet": "At nonparallel density-one directions \\(a\\) of \\(A\\) and \\(b\\) of \\(B\\), the point \\(x+a+b\\) has density one of \\(X\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Assume the weak measurable five-coloring and palettes of Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">A state is a measurable angular set together with a specified nonempty group of labels contained in its palette almost everywhere.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(A\\subset S\\), define\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{ess\\,supp}_S A=\n  \\{a:\\sigma(A\\cap I)&gt;0\\text{ for every open arc }I\\ni a\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let states \\(A,B\\) at \\(x\\) represent disjoint label groups whose union is \\(\\mathcal I\\setminus\\{X\\}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At nonparallel density-one directions \\(a\\) of \\(A\\) and \\(b\\) of \\(B\\), the point \\(x+a+b\\) has density one of \\(X\\).</p><p>If \\(\\sigma(A)&gt;0\\), the closed essential support of \\(B\\) contains no unit chord, and therefore has strict angular separation from its \\(R\\)- and \\(R^{-1}\\)-images.</p><p>This separation is uniform along sequences \\((x_n,A_n,B_n)\\) representing the same groups if \\(1_{A_n}\\to1_A\\) in \\(L^1(S)\\) for a positive-measure \\(A\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The density assertion of Proposition 4 excludes the four represented labels at the sum, leaving density one of \\(X\\).</p><p>For uniform separation, suppose support pairs have chord lengths tending to one. Approximate support points by density directions to obtain \\(b_n,d_n\\) in \\(B_n\\) with\n\\[\nb_n\\to b,\\quad d_n\\to d,\\quad\nw_n=b_n-d_n\\to w=b-d,\\quad |w|=1.\n\\]\nChoose an angular interval \\(I\\) with \\(\\sigma(A\\cap I)&gt;0\\), whose closure avoids directions parallel to \\(w,b,d\\). The implicit function theorem gives unit directions \\(c_n(a)\\), \\(a\\in I\\), satisfying\n\\[\n|w_n+a-c_n(a)|=1,\\qquad c_n\\to\\operatorname{id}\\text{ in }C^1(I).\n\\]\nThe derivative in the angle of \\(c_n\\), at the limiting solution, is \\(-2w\\cdot Ja\\ne0\\).</p><p>Uniform inverse-derivative bounds, change of variables, and continuous approximation give\n\\[\n1_{A_n}\\circ c_n\\to1_A\n\\quad\\text{in }L^1(I).\n\\]\nThus for large \\(n\\), a positive-measure set has both \\(a,c_n(a)\\in A_n\\). Choose both to be density directions. The pairs with \\(b_n,d_n\\) are nonparallel. Hence\n\\[\nx_n+a+b_n,\\qquad x_n+c_n(a)+d_n\n\\]\nboth have density one of \\(X\\), while their distance is one. This contradicts Proposition 3. The fixed-center conclusion follows from a constant sequence, and compactness turns exclusion into a strict margin.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Lemma 6.4, Singleton–pair alternation is impossible", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Such a state function cannot change state under \\(R\\) almost everywhere.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Assume the weak measurable five-coloring and palettes of Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">At one center, suppose a measurable state function has only two values: a singleton \\(\\{X\\}\\) and a fixed disjoint pair \\(D\\), each group contained in the palette on its state set.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Such a state function cannot change state under \\(R\\) almost everywhere.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put\n\\[\n\\gamma=2\\arcsin\\frac1{2\\sqrt3},\\qquad \\cos\\gamma=\\frac56.\n\\]\nThe ratio \\(\\gamma/\\pi\\) is irrational: otherwise \\(e^{i\\gamma}\\) would be a root of unity, making the rational algebraic integer \\(2\\cos\\gamma=5/3\\) an integer.</p><p>A positive-measure set has equal states at \\(t,t+\\gamma\\). Otherwise the state flips under \\(\\gamma\\), is invariant under \\(2\\gamma\\), and hence is almost everywhere constant by irrational-rotation Fourier analysis, contradicting the \\(R\\)-flip.</p><p>Choose such \\(t\\), also satisfying all finitely many needed alternation and density conditions. For \\(j=0,1,2\\), put\n\\[\nz_j=x+e^{i(t+2j\\kappa)}\n+e^{i(t+\\pi+\\gamma+2j\\kappa)}\n=x+e^{i(t+2j\\kappa)}(1-e^{i\\gamma}).\n\\]\nThese points lie at radius \\(1/\\sqrt3\\) and form a unit equilateral triangle. In each sum, the endpoint states are opposite, since \\(2\\kappa\\) preserves state and \\(\\pi=3\\kappa\\) flips it. Proposition 4 gives density one at each \\(z_j\\) to the two labels outside \\(\\{X\\}\\cup D\\).</p><p>A common small translation puts all three vertices in that union and in the typical set: each failure probability tends to zero. The resulting proper unit triangle would use two labels, a contradiction.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Theorem 6.5, Local finiteness of cyclic centers", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The set \\(\\mathcal L\\) is closed and locally finite:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Assume the weak measurable five-coloring and transition graphs of Propositions 4 and 7.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal L=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^2:\\Gamma_x\\text{ contains a cycle}\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The set \\(\\mathcal L\\) is closed and locally finite: its intersection with every compact plane set is finite.</p>", "proof": "<p>Each specified simple-cycle locus is the intersection of finitely many closed edge-presence loci. There are finitely many simple cycles on five labels, so \\(\\mathcal L\\) is closed.</p><p>For a triangle, Proposition 7 and palette size at most two force every palette to meet the fixed outside pair: every nonempty subset of its three labels of size at most two lies in a triangle edge. The center is binary for that outside pair, so Proposition 5 gives local finiteness.</p><p>Lemma 10.2 rules out accumulation for each four-cycle locus; Lemma 10.4 does so for each five-cycle locus. Each closed locus therefore has finite intersection with compact sets. Their finite union has the same property.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.6, Continuity of four-cycle states", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\(s\\in\\{X,U,V\\}\\), the map \\( x\\mapsto1_{\\{w_x=s\\}} \\) is continuous from \\(L\\) to \\(L^1(S)\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a four-cycle labeled \\(0,1,2,3\\) cyclically; let \\(X\\) be the fifth label and \\(L\\) its edge-presence locus.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(U=\\{0,2\\}\\), \\(V=\\{1,3\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(x\\in L\\), define \\(w_x(e)=X\\) if \\(X\\in P_x(e)\\); otherwise let \\(w_x(e)=U\\) or \\(V\\) according to which pair equals \\(P_x(e)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">This is exhaustive almost everywhere: Proposition 7 excludes all cycle singletons and adjacent pairs, while Proposition 6 bounds size by two.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(s\\in\\{X,U,V\\}\\), the map\n\\[\nx\\mapsto1_{\\{w_x=s\\}}\n\\]\nis continuous from \\(L\\) to \\(L^1(S)\\). Both diagonal states have positive angular measure, and \\(w_x(e)\\ne w_x(Re)\\) almost everywhere.</p>", "proof": "<p>State change follows from palette disjointness. If one diagonal were null, the other diagonal and \\(X\\) would contradict Proposition 9.</p><p>At each \\(y\\in L\\), choose an avoiding trace \\(b_y^{ij}\\) for each specified edge. If \\(k\\) belongs to the group represented by state \\(s\\), then\n\\[\n1_{\\{w_y=s\\}}\\le\\sum_{ij\\text{ in the cycle}}b_{y,k}^{ij}\n\\quad\\text{almost everywhere}.\n\\]\nAt a generic direction, choose an edge containing the other possible palette member but not \\(k\\). For a diagonal choose an edge incident to its other member; for \\(k=X\\), choose one incident to the additional label. If the palette is just \\(\\{k\\}\\), any edge suffices. Its avoiding trace must then have \\(k\\)-coordinate one.</p><p>For \\(y_n\\to x\\), take simultaneous weak-* limits of the three state indicators and four traces. Trace limits belong to \\(\\mathcal W_x\\). Where a limiting state weight is positive, the displayed inequality puts every label of that group into \\(P_x\\). Two state groups would require at least three labels. Thus precisely one limiting state weight is one almost everywhere. Its group identifies the canonical state at \\(x\\).</p><p>Weak-* convergence of indicators to an indicator implies \\(L^1\\)-convergence by testing against \\(1\\) and the limiting indicator. Every subsequence has a further subsequence with this limit, proving continuity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The locus \\(L\\) has no accumulation point.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the fixed four-cycle locus \\(L\\), states \\(U,V,X\\), and essential supports\n  \\[\n  S_{U,x}=\\operatorname{ess\\,supp}_S\\{w_x=U\\},\n  \\quad\n  S_{V,x}=\\operatorname{ess\\,supp}_S\\{w_x=V\\},\n  \\quad N_x=S_{U,x}\\cup S_{V,x}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The locus \\(L\\) has no accumulation point.</p>", "proof": "<p>By Proposition 8, each diagonal support has strict separation from its own \\(R\\)-shift; the margins are uniform along convergent center sequences by Lemma 10.1. That continuity also gives: if \\(b\\in S_{s,x}\\) and \\(y_n\\to x\\) in \\(L\\), then some \\(b_n\\in S_{s,y_n}\\) tend to \\(b\\).</p><p>The set \\(N_x\\) is proper. If it covered \\(S\\), an intersection point of its two nonempty supports would rotate outside both, which is impossible; if the supports were disjoint, they would disconnect \\(S\\). On each gap of \\(S\\setminus N_x\\), the state is \\(X\\) almost everywhere. Such a gap has length at most \\(\\kappa\\), by state change under \\(R\\).</p><p>For nonparallel \\(a,b\\), let\n\\[\nT_h(a,b)=(c,d),\\qquad c+d=a+b-h,\n\\]\nbe the nearby inverse-sum branch. If \\(y=x+h\\in L\\), support transport gives\n\\[\na\\in S_{U,x},\\ b\\in S_{V,x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nT_h(a,b)\\in N_y\\times N_y.\n\\]\nIndeed, approximate by positive-measure sets of density directions. Their sums have density one of \\(X\\). The local diffeomorphism permits removal of null output directions. Neither output state can be \\(X\\), by density exclusion. Closedness then gives the rule for support points. It also holds with the states interchanged and in reverse under \\(T_{-h}\\).</p><p>Suppose \\(y_n=x+h_n\\to x\\) are distinct points of \\(L\\). Write \\(\\delta_n=|h_n|\\) and pass to \\(h_n/\\delta_n\\to H\\in S\\). Let \\(a\\) be a right-gap endpoint of \\(N_x\\), say \\(a\\in S_{U,x}\\), and suppose \\(H\\cdot a\\ne0\\). The first output angle has velocity\n\\[\nV_H(a;b)\n=-\\frac{H\\cdot b}{a\\times b}\n=-H\\cdot Ja-(H\\cdot a)\\cot(\\arg b-\\arg a).\n\\]\nPositive measure of the \\(V\\)-state gives nonparallel \\(b,b'\\in S_{V,x}\\) with \\(V_H(a;b)&gt;V_H(a;b')\\).</p><p>Let \\(c_n=T^1_{h_n}(a,b)\\in N_{y_n}\\). Then\n\\[\n\\arg c_n=\\arg a+\\delta_nV_H(a;b)+o(\\delta_n).\n\\]\nIf \\(c_n\\in S_{U,y_n}\\) infinitely often, choose \\(b'_n\\in S_{V,y_n}\\to b'\\) and transport back. The first output lies in \\(N_x\\) at angle\n\\[\n\\arg a+\\delta_n\\bigl(V_H(a;b)-V_H(a;b')\\bigr)+o(\\delta_n),\n\\]\nstrictly inside the gap, a contradiction. No convergence rate for \\(b'_n\\) is needed: for the first-angle function \\(\\tau\\),\n\\[\n\\tau(h,\\theta,\\beta)\n=\\theta+D_h\\tau(0,\\theta,\\beta)h+O(|h|^2),\n\\]\nso changes in \\(\\beta\\) affect only a coefficient multiplied by \\(h\\).</p><p>Hence \\(c_n\\in S_{V,y_n}\\) eventually. Strict separation for \\(U\\) leaves a neighborhood of \\(Ra\\) free of \\(U\\). The gap immediately right of \\(a\\) is \\(X\\), so immediately right of \\(Ra\\) the state is non-\\(X\\), hence \\(V\\). Therefore \\(Ra\\in S_{V,x}\\), and continuity gives \\(V\\)-support points at \\(y_n\\) tending to \\(Ra\\). Together with \\(c_n\\to a\\), these contradict uniform strict separation for \\(V\\).</p><p>For a left gap, reverse the velocity inequality. Thus every gap endpoint must satisfy \\(H\\cdot a=0\\). These are only two antipodal directions. A nonempty gap has two distinct endpoints and length at most \\(\\kappa&lt;\\pi\\), so both cannot be those directions. This rules out accumulation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.3", "orig": "Lemma 6.7, A support contact bans an angular step", "depth": 1, "snippet": "If \\(i,i+1\\in S(a)\\), then the steps \\(i\\to i+1\\) and \\(i+1\\to i\\) under \\(R\\) are absent almost everywhere on the entire angular circle at \\(x\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a five-cycle with labels \\(\\mathbb Z/5\\mathbb Z\\), and let \\(x\\) belong to its locus.</p><p class=\"li\">Almost every palette is a diagonal\n  \\[\n  D_i=\\{i,i+2\\}.\n  \\]\n  Write \\(w_x(e)=i\\) for this state.</p><p class=\"li\">Then\n  \\[\n  w_x(Re)=w_x(e)\\pm1\\pmod5\n  \\]\n  almost everywhere.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S_i\\) be its closed essential state support and let \\(S(a)=\\{i:a\\in S_i\\}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If \\(i,i+1\\in S(a)\\), then the steps \\(i\\to i+1\\) and \\(i+1\\to i\\) under \\(R\\) are absent almost everywhere on the entire angular circle at \\(x\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Set \\(z=x+a\\). State-\\(i\\) directions occur with positive measure in every neighborhood of \\(a\\). Palette exclusion, nonsingularity of the sum map, and Fubini give typical actual unit-circle samplings at centers approaching \\(z\\) that avoid \\(D_i\\). A weak-* limit is a trace at \\(z\\) avoiding \\(D_i\\). Separately obtain one avoiding \\(D_{i+1}\\). Thus\n\\[\nP_z(f)\\not\\subset D_i,\\qquad P_z(f)\\not\\subset D_{i+1}\n\\]\nalmost everywhere.</p><p>Transport through \\(z+f=x+f+a\\) gives \\(P_z(f)\\cap P_x(f)=\\varnothing\\), except on a null set. Let \\(X\\) be the label outside \\(D_i\\cup D_{i+1}\\). If \\(w_x(f)=i\\), disjointness leaves \\(D_{i+1}\\cup\\{X\\}\\), and the avoiding trace forces \\(X\\in P_z(f)\\). The same holds for state \\(i+1\\). A step between them would put \\(X\\) in both \\(P_z(f)\\) and \\(P_z(Rf)\\), contrary to sixty-degree exclusion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The locus of the specified five-cycle has no accumulation point.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the five-cycle states and supports in Lemma 10.3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The locus of the specified five-cycle has no accumulation point.</p>", "proof": "<p>The finitely many state supports cover \\(S\\), so each support list is nonempty. Each nonempty \\(S_i\\) has strict separation from its own \\(R\\)-shift: an adjacent state has positive measure by the step rule, and their two diagonals cover four labels, so Proposition 8 applies. Hence\n\\[\nS(a)\\cap S(Ra)=\\varnothing.\n\\]\nAlso, each \\(i\\in S(a)\\) has a neighbor in both \\(S(Ra)\\) and \\(S(R^{-1}a)\\), by approaching \\(a\\) through generic state-\\(i\\) directions.</p><p>Suppose distinct \\(y_n=x+h_n\\to x\\), and pass to \\(h_n/|h_n|\\to H\\). Write\n\\[\ny_n=x+a_n^++a_n^-,\n\\qquad\na_n^\\pm=\\frac{h_n}{2}\n\\pm\\sqrt{1-\\frac{|h_n|^2}{4}}\\,J\\frac{h_n}{|h_n|}.\n\\]\nThese pairs are nonparallel, with limits \\(a=JH\\) and \\(-a\\).</p><p>For either endpoint \\(s=a_n^\\pm\\), the diagonals indexed by \\(S(s)\\) have empty common intersection. Otherwise a common label \\(k\\) occurs in almost every nearby palette, because the other finitely many closed supports can be excluded. Palette exclusion and the local sum diffeomorphism then make \\(A_k\\) null in an open neighborhood of \\(y_n\\). But \\(y_n\\in H_k\\) for every label by its five cycle edges and Lemma 7.1.</p><p>Closedness and finiteness give \\(S(v)\\subset S(u)\\) near fixed \\(u\\), so the empty-intersection property passes to \\(a,-a\\). Such a list contains adjacent indices: otherwise it is an independent set of the index five-cycle, of size at most two, and its diagonals have a common label.</p><p>Put \\(S^{(r)}=S(R^ra)\\), \\(r\\in\\mathbb Z/6\\mathbb Z\\). Relabel so \\(0,1\\in S^{(0)}\\). Disjointness and the neighbor condition force\n\\[\n2,4\\in S^{(1)}\\cap S^{(5)}.\n\\]\nIf \\(j,j+1\\in S^{(3)}\\), they similarly force\n\\[\nj-1,j+2\\in S^{(2)}\\cap S^{(4)}.\n\\]\nThose indices cannot be \\(2,4\\); checking residues leaves \\(j=1\\) or \\(4\\). In the first case, contacts \\(\\{0,1\\}\\) and \\(\\{1,2\\}\\) ban both steps from state \\(1\\); in the second, contacts \\(\\{0,1\\}\\) and \\(\\{4,0\\}\\) ban both steps from state \\(0\\). Lemma 10.3 and the step rule make the shared state null, contradicting its support membership. Only two fixed contact directions were used.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Proposition 7.1, A cycle of common exclusion continua", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There exist \\(\\ell\\in\\{3,4,5\\}\\), distinct labels \\(i_0,\\ldots,i_{\\ell-1}\\), a point \\(x\\), and \\(\\rho>0\\), such that for each cyclic edge \\(i_ji_{j+1}\\) there are a compact connected …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Assume the weak measurable five-coloring, supports \\(H_i\\), and graphs \\(\\Gamma_x\\) defined above.</p><p class=\"li\">For nonempty compact \\(K\\subset\\mathbb R^2\\), define\n  \\[\n  \\Delta(K)=\n  \\left\\{z:\\min_{q\\in K}|z-q|&lt;1&lt;\\max_{q\\in K}|z-q|\\right\\}.\n  \\]\n  This is open, since the distance extrema are \\(1\\)-Lipschitz.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exist \\(\\ell\\in\\{3,4,5\\}\\), distinct labels \\(i_0,\\ldots,i_{\\ell-1}\\), a point \\(x\\), and \\(\\rho&gt;0\\), such that for each cyclic edge \\(i_ji_{j+1}\\) there are a compact connected\n\\[\nK_j\\subset\\overline{B(x,\\rho)},\n\\]\na direction \\(v_j\\in S\\), and points \\(q_{j,k}\\in K_j\\setminus\\{x\\}\\) satisfying:\n\\[\nx\\in K_j,\\qquad K_j\\cap\\partial B(x,\\rho)\\ne\\varnothing;\n\\]\n\\[\nq_{j,k}\\to x,\\qquad\n\\frac{q_{j,k}-x}{|q_{j,k}-x|}\\to v_j;\n\\]\n\\[\nH_{i_j}\\cap\\Delta(K_j)\n=\nH_{i_{j+1}}\\cap\\Delta(K_j)\n=\\varnothing.\n\\]\nThe extracted cycle need not consist of edges of \\(\\Gamma_x\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use Lemmas 11.3 and 11.5. Since \\(\\mathcal L_0\\cap Q\\) is finite, pass to a subsequence with fixed \\(x\\) whose core boundary is non-null. Choose \\(\\rho&gt;0\\) so \\(\\overline{B(x,\\rho)}\\subset\\operatorname{int}Q\\) contains no other point of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\). For large \\(n\\), the core \\(B_{n,x}\\) lies inside, and all other core closures are outside. Thus \\(g_n\\) is defined on\n\\[\nA_n=\\overline{B(x,\\rho)}\\setminus B_{n,x}.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(W_n\\) represent its non-null inner loop. Lemma 11.6 gives a simple cycle in \\(W_n\\); pass to a subsequence with one fixed simple label cycle. For each of its edge midpoints \\(m_j\\), Lemma 11.7 gives a compact connected\n\\[\nK_{n,j}\\subset g_n^{-1}(\\{m_j\\})\\cap A_n\n\\]\nmeeting both boundaries.</p><p>Take simultaneous Hausdorff limits \\(K_{n,j}\\to K_j\\). The hyperspace of nonempty compact subsets of the compact disk is compact: finite nets give total boundedness, and limits of convergent point selections give completeness. Connectedness passes to the limit, since separated neighborhoods of a hypothetical disconnection would disconnect sufficiently close approximants. Inner contacts tend to \\(x\\), because core diameters vanish; outer contacts persist. Thus \\(x\\in K_j\\) and \\(K_j\\) meets the outer circle.</p><p>At points of \\(K_{n,j}\\) outside holes, \\(q^{\\varepsilon_n}=m_j\\), so either endpoint label \\(i\\) has \\(p_i^{\\varepsilon_n}&gt;\\alpha\\). Fix a positive-radius closed ball \\(U\\subset\\Delta(K_j)\\). Hausdorff convergence preserves its uniform strict distance-straddling margins. Connectedness of \\(K_{n,j}\\) supplies an exact unit neighbor of every \\(z\\in U\\). If \\(z\\notin E_n\\), that neighbor is outside all closed holes. Hence\n\\[\np_i^{\\varepsilon_n}(z)\\le1-\\alpha.\n\\]\nThe same estimate as in Lemma 11.5 yields \\(|A_i\\cap U|=0\\). Every point of \\(\\Delta(K_j)\\) has such a neighborhood, so \\(H_i\\cap\\Delta(K_j)=\\varnothing\\), for both endpoint labels.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\n\\{|q-x|:q\\in K_j\\}=[0,\\rho]\n\\]\nby connectedness and the contacts. Choose points at radii \\(\\rho/k\\), pass to a convergent subsequence of their directions, and obtain \\(v_j,q_{j,k}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.1", "orig": "Lemma 7.2, Disk inequalities and support detection", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every exact unit pair \\(q,z\\), \\( p_i^\\varepsilon(q)+p_i^\\varepsilon(z)\\le1. \\) If \\(q_n\\to q\\), \\(\\varepsilon_n\\to0\\), and \\(\\liminf_np_i^{\\varepsilon_n}(q_n)>0\\), then \\(q\\in H_i\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), define\n  \\[\n  p_i^\\varepsilon(q)=\n  \\frac1{\\pi\\varepsilon^2}\\int_{B(0,\\varepsilon)}a_i(q+v)\\,dv.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">These are continuous probability coordinates, with\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{Lip}(p_i^\\varepsilon)\\le C/\\varepsilon,\\qquad\n  p_i^\\varepsilon\\to a_i\\text{ in }L^1_{\\rm loc}\n  \\]\n  and almost everywhere as \\(\\varepsilon\\downarrow0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every exact unit pair \\(q,z\\),\n\\[\np_i^\\varepsilon(q)+p_i^\\varepsilon(z)\\le1.\n\\]\nIf \\(q_n\\to q\\), \\(\\varepsilon_n\\to0\\), and\n\\(\\liminf_np_i^{\\varepsilon_n}(q_n)&gt;0\\), then \\(q\\in H_i\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Translate the exact unit pair by a uniform vector in \\(B(0,\\varepsilon)\\). Almost every translation puts both points in the typical set, where they cannot both have label \\(i\\). Averaging proves the pointwise inequality.</p><p>Every neighborhood of \\(q\\) eventually contains \\(B(q_n,\\varepsilon_n)\\). A positive lower bound on its average gives positive \\(A_i\\)-area there, proving support membership.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.2", "orig": "Lemma 7.3, A missing edge has small overlap", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a ball \\(B\\) about \\(x\\) such that, whenever \\(\\overline{B'}\\subset B\\), \\( \\int_{B'}p_i^\\varepsilon(q)p_j^\\varepsilon(q)\\,dq=o(\\varepsilon). \\) For \\(\\overline{B''}\\subset B'\\) and fixed \\(\\alpha>0\\), the set …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the disk averages of Lemma 11.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(ij\\notin\\Gamma_x\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a ball \\(B\\) about \\(x\\) such that, whenever \\(\\overline{B'}\\subset B\\),\n\\[\n\\int_{B'}p_i^\\varepsilon(q)p_j^\\varepsilon(q)\\,dq=o(\\varepsilon).\n\\]\nFor \\(\\overline{B''}\\subset B'\\) and fixed \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\), the set\n\\[\n\\{q\\in B'':p_i^\\varepsilon(q)&gt;\\alpha,\\ p_j^\\varepsilon(q)&gt;\\alpha\\}\n\\]\nhas finite open-disk covers with total radii tending to zero. Every covered point lies in a disk interior.</p>", "proof": "<p>Nonedge presence gives \\(D_{ij}(B,h)=o(|h|)\\). Put\n\\[\n\\omega(r)=\\sup_{0&lt;|h|\\le r}\\frac{|D_{ij}(B,h)|}{|h|}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nExpand the disk-average product using independent \\(h_1,h_2\\in B(0,\\varepsilon)\\). For \\(\\varepsilon&lt;\\operatorname{dist}(\\overline{B'},\\mathbb R^2\\setminus B)\\), changing root to \\(q+h_1\\) bounds the relevant transition area by\n\\[\n|D_{ij}(B,h_2-h_1)|\\le2\\varepsilon\\omega(2\\varepsilon).\n\\]\nAt equal shifts the simultaneous event is empty. Fubini gives the overlap bound.</p><p>Take grid squares of side \\(l=c_\\alpha\\varepsilon\\), with \\(c_\\alpha\\) small enough that both averages oscillate by at most \\(\\alpha/2\\) on a square. Select all squares touching the violation set in \\(B''\\). For small \\(\\varepsilon\\), these lie in \\(B'\\), with both averages at least \\(\\alpha/2\\) throughout. If their number is \\(N_\\varepsilon\\), disjoint interiors give\n\\[\nN_\\varepsilon l^2\\alpha^2/4=o(\\varepsilon),\n\\qquad N_\\varepsilon\\varepsilon=o(1).\n\\]\nThe open disk of radius \\(l\\) about each square center strictly contains its closed square. Total radii are \\(N_\\varepsilon l=o(1)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.3", "orig": "Lemma 7.4, Small holes and tree fillings", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are \\(\\varepsilon_n\\downarrow0\\) and finite families \\(\\mathcal H_n\\) of open disks with pairwise disjoint closures and total radii tending to zero such that, for large \\(n\\):", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(0&lt;\\alpha&lt;1/5\\), and define\n  \\[\n  q_i^\\varepsilon(z)=\n  \\frac{(p_i^\\varepsilon(z)-\\alpha)_+}\n  {\\sum_{k\\in\\mathcal I}(p_k^\\varepsilon(z)-\\alpha)_+}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal G\\) be the one-skeleton of the simplex on the five labels, realized as the complete graph.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose a closed square \\(Q\\) of side ten with \\(\\partial Q\\cap\\mathcal L=\\varnothing\\), a larger square \\(Q'\\) containing \\(Q\\) in its interior, and \\(\\mathcal L_0=\\mathcal L\\cap Q'\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(\\varepsilon_n\\downarrow0\\) and finite families \\(\\mathcal H_n\\) of open disks with pairwise disjoint closures and total radii tending to zero such that, for large \\(n\\):</p><p class=\"li num\">1. every \\(x\\in\\mathcal L_0\\) has exactly one designated core disk \\(B_{n,x}\\) containing it; different points have different cores;</p><p class=\"li num\">2. cores for \\(x\\in Q\\) lie inside \\(\\operatorname{int}Q\\), while cores for \\(x\\notin Q\\) have closures disjoint from \\(Q\\);</p><p class=\"li num\">3. \\(q^{\\varepsilon_n}\\) is \\(\\mathcal G\\)-valued outside all holes in \\(Q\\);</p><p class=\"li num\">4. filling noncore holes through trees gives a continuous\n   \\[\n   g_n:Q\\setminus\\bigcup_{x\\in\\mathcal L_0\\cap Q}B_{n,x}\n   \\longrightarrow\\mathcal G\n   \\]\n   equal to \\(q^{\\varepsilon_n}\\) outside all open holes.</p>", "proof": "<p>For small fixed \\(\\delta&gt;0\\), start with core disks \\(B(x,\\delta)\\), \\(x\\in\\mathcal L_0\\), and set\n\\[\nM_\\delta=Q'\\setminus\\bigcup_xB(x,\\delta).\n\\]\nIt is compact and every \\(\\Gamma_z\\), \\(z\\in M_\\delta\\), is a forest.</p><p>For each \\(z\\), use Lemma 11.2 for all nonedges of \\(\\Gamma_z\\), choosing a common radius \\(s_z\\) with transition estimates on \\(B(z,3s_z)\\), overlap estimates on \\(B(z,2s_z)\\), and covers for violations in \\(B(z,s_z)\\). Choose finitely many such balls covering \\(M_\\delta\\), with Lebesgue number \\(\\lambda_\\delta&gt;0\\). The marked violation disks have total radii \\(m_{\\delta,\\varepsilon}\\to0\\).</p><p>Outside marked disks, positive threshold coordinates form a clique in each covering forest, hence have size at most two and, if two, form a forest edge.</p><p>Merge any two disks with intersecting closures into an open disk strictly containing both closures, of radius at most the sum plus a chosen error. There are finitely many merges; total errors can be below any \\(\\eta&gt;0\\). Final disks have disjoint closures and total radii at most\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L_0|\\delta+m_{\\delta,\\varepsilon}+\\eta.\n\\]\nA final noncore radius is at most \\(m_{\\delta,\\varepsilon}+\\eta\\).</p><p>Take \\(\\delta_n\\downarrow0\\), then choose \\(\\varepsilon_n\\) and errors so that\n\\[\n\\varepsilon_n&lt;\\min\\{1/n,\\varepsilon_{n-1}/2\\},\\qquad\nm_{\\delta_n,\\varepsilon_n}+\\eta_n\n&lt;\\min\\{1/n,\\lambda_{\\delta_n}/3\\}.\n\\]\nFor large \\(n\\), small diameters prevent a disk from containing two points of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\), and positive distances to \\(\\partial Q\\) give the core positions.</p><p>A final noncore closed disk meeting \\(Q\\) lies inside \\(Q'\\), outside original cores, and has diameter below \\(\\lambda_{\\delta_n}\\). It is contained in one selected forest ball. Its boundary avoids all original marked interiors, so its \\(q^{\\varepsilon_n}\\)-image lies in that forest, hence in one tree. Contractibility extends the boundary map across the disk through that tree. If the disk crosses \\(\\partial Q\\), extend over the whole disk and restrict. Finite pasting gives \\(g_n\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.4", "orig": "Lemma 7.5, A cover obstruction on a square", "depth": 1, "snippet": "A closed square of side ten has no relatively open cover of multiplicity at most two and mesh at most two.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">The multiplicity of a cover is the maximum number of members containing a point.</p><p class=\"li\">Its mesh is the supremum of member diameters.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A closed square of side ten has no relatively open cover of multiplicity at most two and mesh at most two.</p>", "proof": "<p>Take a finite subcover \\(U_1,\\ldots,U_N\\), choose \\(z_a\\in U_a\\), and form a partition of unity \\(\\phi_a\\) subordinate to it using normalized distances to \\(Q\\setminus U_a\\). No member equals \\(Q\\), by the mesh bound.</p><p>Define\n\\[\nF(z)=\\sum_a\\phi_a(z)z_a.\n\\]\nIt maps \\(Q\\) into itself. At most two weights are positive, so its image lies in finitely many segments and vertices. Moreover,\n\\[\n|F(z)-z|\\le\\sum_a\\phi_a(z)|z_a-z|\\le2.\n\\]\nFor \\(y\\) with \\(\\operatorname{dist}(y,\\partial Q)&gt;2\\), the map \\(z\\mapsto y+z-F(z)\\) maps \\(Q\\) into itself. Brouwer's theorem gives a fixed point, hence \\(F(z)=y\\). Thus the image contains the nonempty open central region, impossible for finitely many segments.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.5", "orig": "Lemma 7.6, Non-null core loops", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For all sufficiently large \\(n\\), at least one core disk contained in \\(Q\\) has a boundary loop whose image under \\(g_n\\) is not null homotopic in \\(\\mathcal G\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the disks and maps of Lemma 11.3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For all sufficiently large \\(n\\), at least one core disk contained in \\(Q\\) has a boundary loop whose image under \\(g_n\\) is not null homotopic in \\(\\mathcal G\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For a closed hole disk \\(\\overline{B(u,r)}\\), \\(r&lt;1\\), any point at distance one from some point of the disk belongs to\n\\[\nE(u,r)=\\{z:1-r\\le|z-u|\\le1+r\\},\n\\qquad |E(u,r)|=4\\pi r.\n\\]\nLet\n\\[\nE_n=\\bigcup_{B(u,r)\\in\\mathcal H_n}E(u,r).\n\\]\nThen \\(|E_n|\\to0\\). Outside \\(E_n\\), no exact unit neighbor lies in any closed hole.</p><p>If all core loops were null along a subsequence, fill them in \\(\\mathcal G\\), obtaining \\(\\widetilde g_n:Q\\to\\mathcal G\\). Components of\n\\[\nU_{n,i}=\\{z\\in Q:(\\widetilde g_n(z))_i&gt;0\\}\n\\]\nare relatively open and path connected, and form a multiplicity-two cover.</p><p>Its mesh is at most \\(1+o(1)\\). Otherwise there are paths in one fixed label set joining points more than \\(1+c\\) apart. Move any endpoint in a hole to the first boundary point along the path, changing it by at most the vanishing hole diameter. The replacement endpoints \\(a_n,b_n\\) satisfy\n\\[\np_i^{\\varepsilon_n}(a_n)&gt;\\alpha,\\qquad\np_i^{\\varepsilon_n}(b_n)&gt;\\alpha.\n\\]\nPass to limits \\(a,b\\), \\(|a-b|&gt;1\\). Lemma 11.1 gives \\(a\\in H_i\\).</p><p>For a small fixed ball \\(U\\) about \\(a\\), distances from \\(z\\in U\\) to \\(a_n,b_n\\) straddle one. The path contains an exact unit neighbor of \\(z\\). For \\(z\\notin E_n\\), such a neighbor is outside all closed holes, with \\(p_i^{\\varepsilon_n}&gt;\\alpha\\). Lemma 11.1 implies\n\\[\np_i^{\\varepsilon_n}(z)\\le1-\\alpha.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\alpha|A_i\\cap U|\n\\le\\|p_i^{\\varepsilon_n}-a_i\\|_{L^1(U)}\n+\\alpha|E_n\\cap U|\\longrightarrow0,\n\\]\ncontrary to \\(a\\in H_i\\).</p><p>The mesh is eventually at most two, contradicting Lemma 11.4. This also excludes the case of no core disks in \\(Q\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.6", "orig": "Lemma 7.7, Midpoint detection by a reduced circuit", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every nontrivial free homotopy class has a nonempty cyclically reduced closed edge word \\(W\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal G\\) be a finite connected simple graph.</p><p class=\"li\">An edge path is reduced if consecutive edges never reverse; a closed reduced word is cyclically reduced if its last and first edges do not reverse either.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every nontrivial free homotopy class has a nonempty cyclically reduced closed edge word \\(W\\). Such a word contains a simple cycle.</p><p>If \\(m\\) is the midpoint of any edge occurring in \\(W\\), every loop freely homotopic to a nonzero power of \\(W\\) meets \\(m\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The covering tree based at a vertex has vertices the reduced paths from it, and edges given by appending and reducing one edge. Deleting the last edge gives the unique route to the empty word, so this is a tree; recording endpoints is a covering. Lift paths and contract to the unique tree path to obtain unique reduced representatives. Compact path images lie in finite subtrees.</p><p>Move a loop's basepoint to a vertex, reduce, and trim inverse first and last edges. The result is cyclically reduced and empty exactly for a null loop. Basepoint changes and free homotopies conjugate the based class.</p><p>Conjugation preserves a nonempty cyclically reduced circuit up to cyclic rotation: reduce the conjugating path \\(P\\) in \\(PWP^{-1}\\). Cancellation at its last edge shortens \\(P\\) and rotates one edge of \\(W\\); cancellation at both ends of \\(W\\) is impossible. If neither end cancels, cyclic trimming removes the conjugating path. Induct on its length.</p><p>Every nonzero power is cyclically reduced and uses every edge of \\(W\\). But \\(\\mathcal G\\setminus\\{m\\}\\) deformation retracts onto the graph with that edge deleted by contracting the half-open stubs. A loop avoiding \\(m\\) has a reduced circuit using no such edge, so cannot represent a nonzero power.</p><p>Finally, a shortest nonempty subloop between repeated vertices has no repeated interior vertex. Simplicity excludes length one, and reduction excludes length two. It is a simple cycle.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.7", "orig": "Lemma 7.8, A connected midpoint preimage crosses the annulus", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The compact set \\(g^{-1}(\\{m\\})\\) has a compact connected component meeting both boundary circles of \\(A\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(B\\) be an open disk containing \\(x\\), with \\(\\overline B\\subset B(x,\\rho)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  A=\\overline{B(x,\\rho)}\\setminus B.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(g:A\\to\\mathcal G\\) be continuous.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose the counterclockwise inner-boundary image has nonempty cyclically reduced word \\(W\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(m\\) be the midpoint of an edge occurring in \\(W\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The compact set \\(g^{-1}(\\{m\\})\\) has a compact connected component meeting both boundary circles of \\(A\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Every ray from \\(x\\) meets \\(\\partial B\\) once at a positive continuously varying radius. Radial interpolation identifies \\(A\\) with a closed annulus, whose fundamental group is \\(\\mathbb Z\\), generated by the counterclockwise inner boundary.</p><p>Put \\(Z=g^{-1}(\\{m\\})\\), \\(I=Z\\cap\\partial B\\), and \\(O=Z\\cap\\partial B(x,\\rho)\\). Suppose no component meets both. In a compact metric space, a component equals the intersection of its clopen neighborhoods: otherwise separate a purported disconnected intersection into compact parts, and use compactness to obtain a smaller clopen neighborhood omitting one part. Thus compactness of \\(I,O\\) gives a clopen partition\n\\[\nZ=Z_1\\sqcup Z_2,\\qquad I\\subset Z_1,\\quad O\\subset Z_2.\n\\]\nIf a contact set is empty, use the corresponding empty or full part.</p><p>The compact set \\(C_1=Z_1\\cup\\overline B\\) lies in the outer open disk and avoids \\(Z_2\\). Choose a sufficiently fine translated square grid with \\(x\\) on no grid line, and take the finite union \\(V\\) of all closed cells meeting \\(C_1\\). Then \\(C_1\\subset\\operatorname{int}V\\), \\(V\\) stays inside the outer disk, and \\(V\\cap Z_2=\\varnothing\\). Hence \\(\\partial V\\subset A\\setminus Z\\).</p><p>Orient selected cell boundaries counterclockwise and cancel shared edges. The remaining balanced directed chain decomposes into closed polygonal loops. Its total winding about \\(x\\) is one, because exactly one selected square contains \\(x\\) in its interior. Some loop has nonzero winding \\(d\\). Its image avoids \\(m\\), but is freely homotopic to \\(W^d\\), contradicting Lemma 11.6.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Lemma 8.1, One-sided strip restrictions", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Both \\(O(e)\\) and \\(I(e)\\) are nonempty for every \\(e\\notin\\mathcal B\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use a cycle and continua from Proposition 11, relabeling cycle vertices cyclically.</p><p class=\"li\">Edge \\(j\\) joins labels \\(j,j+1\\), with selected limiting direction \\(v_j\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal B\\subset S\\) be the finite antipodally symmetric set perpendicular to at least one \\(v_j\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(e\\notin\\mathcal B\\), give edge \\(j\\) sign \\(+\\) or \\(-\\) according to \\(e\\cdot v_j&gt;0\\) or \\(&lt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(O(e)\\) be the labels remaining after removing endpoints of positive edges, and \\(I(e)\\) those remaining after removing endpoints of negative edges. Outside labels belong to both.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Both \\(O(e)\\) and \\(I(e)\\) are nonempty for every \\(e\\notin\\mathcal B\\). On an angular neighborhood of \\(e\\), an open strip immediately outside radius one uses only \\(O(e)\\), and an open strip immediately inside uses only \\(I(e)\\), up to plane-null sets.</p><p>Moreover,\n\\[\nP_x(e)\\cap O(e)\\ne\\varnothing,\\qquad\nP_x(e)\\cap I(e)\\ne\\varnothing\n\\]\nfor almost every \\(e\\notin\\mathcal B\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Write selected continuum points as\n\\[\nq_{j,n}=x+r_{j,n}w_{j,n},\\qquad r_{j,n}\\downarrow0,\\quad w_{j,n}\\to v_j.\n\\]\nFor a positive edge,\n\\[\n|x+e-q_{j,n}|^2\n=1+r_{j,n}^2-2r_{j,n}(e\\cdot w_{j,n})&lt;1\n\\]\nfor some large fixed \\(n\\). This remains true on a small angular neighborhood and slightly outside radius one. Such points are farther than one from \\(x\\in K_j\\), so lie in \\(\\Delta(K_j)\\). Both endpoint supports are excluded. For a negative edge, the same expression exceeds one, giving an inner strip. Intersect the finitely many neighborhoods and widths. Empty allowed sets would exclude every color on an open set.</p><p>Cover \\(S\\setminus\\mathcal B\\) by countably many smaller constant-sign arcs with positive strip widths. Polar Fubini gives the appropriate restrictions for almost every radius in each width interval. Intersect these countably many radial full-measure conditions and typical-sampling conditions. Choose radii tending to one from above. A weak-* subsequential trace has all excluded coordinates zero on each arc and sum one; it supplies an outer allowed palette member. A separate sequence from below supplies an inner member.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "Proposition 8.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The extracted cycle cannot have length five.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Assume the weak measurable five-coloring and an extracted cycle with the continua of Proposition 11.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The extracted cycle cannot have length five.</p>", "proof": "<p>Index labels modulo five. A label is in \\(I(e)\\) exactly when both incident signs are positive, and in \\(O(e)\\) exactly when both are negative. Nonemptiness requires consecutive signs of each kind. There can be only one run of each kind: two runs of each would require at least six positions. Thus the runs have lengths two and three.</p><p>The two allowed sets are disjoint, of sizes one and two. Palette size at most two forces a pair with one member from each. Let \\(D_i=\\{i,i+2\\}\\). If the length-two run occupies edges \\(0,1\\), the middle label is \\(1\\), and the other run has internal labels \\(3,4\\). The two palette options are \\(D_1,D_4\\), whose indices differ by two. Reversing every sign preserves the options, so antipodal positions have the same two options.</p><p>Choose a full six-position \\(R\\)-orbit on which all assertions hold. If \\(p_j\\) indexes its palette \\(D_{p_j}\\), disjointness of successive palettes gives steps\n\\[\np_{j+1}-p_j=\\varepsilon_j\\in\\{-1,1\\}\\pmod5.\n\\]\nAntipodal differences are \\(0,\\pm2\\) from their common options. Three-step sums are \\(-3,-1,1,3\\); only \\(\\pm3\\) give \\(\\pm2\\pmod5\\), so every consecutive triple of steps must have equal signs. Overlapping triples force all six signs equal, but six equal steps do not close modulo five.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "Proposition 8.5", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The extracted cycle cannot have length four.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Assume the weak measurable five-coloring and an extracted four-cycle from Proposition 11.</p><p class=\"li\">Label it \\(0,1,2,3\\), with outsider \\(X\\), and put \\(U=\\{0,2\\}\\), \\(V=\\{1,3\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A sign pattern is split if it consists of a cyclic run of two positive and two negative edges.</p><p class=\"li\">When \\(X\\notin P_x(e)\\), Proposition 12 forces a split and a diagonal \\(d(e)\\in\\{U,V\\}\\), with \\(d(-e)=d(e)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define the effective state \\(p(e)=X\\) if \\(X\\in P_x(e)\\); otherwise \\(p(e)=d(e)\\). Each represented group is contained in the palette.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The extracted cycle cannot have length four.</p>", "proof": "<p>If one diagonal state were null, the remaining singleton-versus-pair system would contradict Proposition 9; a single state already contradicts state change. Thus both have positive measure, and Lemmas 14.1–14.2 apply.</p><p>Let \\(h(t)\\in\\mathbb Z/2\\mathbb Z\\) be the parity of the \\(X\\)-positions. Suppose \\(t,t'\\in G\\) are within \\(\\eta\\), but \\(h(t)\\ne h(t')\\). Choose the three non-\\(X\\) positions from each word. Around the six positions these alternate between words, and cross-word adjacency forces different diagonals. With only \\(U,V\\), the first word's selected entries all have one value and the second's the other.</p><p>For either word, the three selected directions project to positions equally spaced by \\(\\kappa\\) on the circumference-\\(\\pi\\) circle. An open arc containing all three has length greater than \\(2\\pi/3\\): its closed complement avoids them and lies strictly inside one gap of length \\(\\pi/3\\). Both diagonal arcs would have this property, contradicting the arc of length at most \\(\\pi/2\\).</p><p>Thus \\(h\\) is locally constant on \\(G\\); the dense-set chain makes it constant. But shifting by \\(\\kappa\\) shifts words by one position and reverses parity, a contradiction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.1", "orig": "Lemma 8.3, Six-position word alternatives", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every word \\(w(t)\\), \\(t\\in G\\), contains \\(X\\), and all its \\(X\\)-positions have the same parity.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 14.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F_U,F_V\\) be the closed essential supports of the diagonal state sets.</p><p class=\"li\">In the case where both diagonal states have positive measure, choose a dense conull angular set \\(G\\), invariant under addition of \\(\\kappa\\), on which all six positions avoid \\(\\mathcal B\\), satisfy the state rules, and lie in their respective essential supports.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  w(t)_j=p(e^{i(t+j\\kappa)}),\\qquad j\\in\\mathbb Z/6\\mathbb Z.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every word \\(w(t)\\), \\(t\\in G\\), contains \\(X\\), and all its \\(X\\)-positions have the same parity. Every direction outside \\(\\mathcal B\\) is split.</p>", "proof": "<p>The palette rules give\n\\[\np(e)\\ne p(Re),\\qquad\np(e)=p(-e)\\text{ if both are non-\\(X\\)}.\n\\]\nProposition 8 gives \\(F_D\\cap RF_D=\\varnothing\\), \\(D=U,V\\), and the analogous inverse-shift separation. Compactness supplies \\(\\eta&gt;0\\) such that for \\(t,t'\\in G\\) less than \\(\\eta\\) apart, a diagonal at any position of \\(w(t)\\) cannot equal a diagonal at an adjacent position of \\(w(t')\\). This is the cross-word restriction.</p><p>A word without \\(X\\) alternates \\(U,V\\), contradicting antipodal equality. If \\(X\\) occurs on both parities, an opposite-parity pair must be antipodal, otherwise adjacent. There are exactly two occurrences; shift them to positions zero and three. The remaining positions give a three-periodic permutation of \\(X,U,V\\).</p><p>Such a word is isolated under the cross-word restriction. In a compatible nearby word, positions zero and three, with original neighbors \\(U,V\\), must be \\(X\\). The remaining positions are non-\\(X\\), and cross-word restrictions force their original diagonal values. Thus nearby words are identical.</p><p>Any two points of dense \\(G\\) can be connected by a finite chain in \\(G\\) with steps below \\(\\eta\\), by subdividing an arc and approximating intermediate points. Isolation would make this word constant throughout \\(G\\). But \\(w(t+\\kappa)\\) is its one-position shift, which changes a three-periodic permutation. This is impossible.</p><p>Hence the \\(X\\)-positions have a unique parity. The other parity has three non-\\(X\\) split directions, one from each antipodal pair. Splitting is antipodally invariant, so all six directions are split. Thus almost every direction is split. Signs are locally constant outside finite \\(\\mathcal B\\), so a nonsplit direction there would give a positive-measure nonsplit neighborhood.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.2", "orig": "Lemma 8.4, Geometry of four split rays", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The four selected rays are two distinct antipodal pairs, with opposite directions in opposite edge positions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 14 and the conclusions of Lemma 14.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume both diagonal states have positive measure.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The four selected rays are two distinct antipodal pairs, with opposite directions in opposite edge positions. On the projective angular circle of circumference \\(\\pi\\), the forced diagonal \\(d\\) equals \\(U\\) on one open arc and \\(V\\) on the complementary open arc. One arc has length at most \\(\\pi/2\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Every generic open semicircle selects exactly two rays with adjacent edge positions. Crossing the perpendicular boundary of one unoriented ray line changes its selected multiplicity from one orientation's count to the opposite's. The total remains two, so these counts are equal. All rays are antipodally paired.</p><p>If only one line occurred, two rays would point each way. The selected positions would be one fixed adjacent pair or its complement, giving only one forced diagonal, contrary to positivity of both states. Thus two distinct lines occur, each with one ray each way.</p><p>A ray on one line can be positive simultaneously with either ray on the other. Its edge position is adjacent to both positions from the other line; the remaining position is opposite. Therefore its antipode occupies the opposite edge position.</p><p>Modulo antipodes, the two perpendicular boundaries divide the circle into two open arcs. Crossing one replaces one selected edge by its opposite and changes the forced diagonal. The two arc lengths sum to \\(\\pi\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "Proposition 8.10", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The extracted cycle cannot have length three.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Assume the weak measurable five-coloring and an extracted triangle from Proposition 11.</p><p class=\"li\">Call its three labels the triangle labels and call its outsiders \\(X,Y\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use \\(\\mathcal B\\) from Proposition 12.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The extracted cycle cannot have length three.</p>", "proof": "<p>The seven points \\(\\mathcal M\\) in Lemma 15.4 form the Moser obstruction. The triples \\(0,A,B\\) and \\(T,A,B\\) are unit equilateral triangles sharing \\(AB\\). Any proper three-coloring forces \\(0,T\\) to share a color. Since\n\\[\n|u|^2=(25+11)/36=1,\n\\]\nthe rotated pair similarly forces \\(0,uT\\) to share a color. But\n\\[\n|T-uT|^2\n=3|1-u|^2\n=3\\frac{1+11}{36}=1,\n\\]\na contradiction.</p><p>The placement multiplier has norm one, since \\(35^2+12^2=37^2\\), and so preserves all unit edges. Lemmas 15.3–15.4 give an open neighborhood of each \\(x+z_g\\) restricted almost everywhere to the triangle labels.</p><p>Choose \\(\\rho&gt;0\\) such that a common translation \\(t\\in B(0,\\rho)\\) keeps every vertex in its neighborhood. For each vertex, translations hitting the atypical set or a nontriangle-label exceptional set form a null set. Avoid their finite union. All seven translated vertices are typical and triangle-labeled, giving a proper three-coloring of the Moser obstruction, impossible.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.1", "orig": "Lemma 8.6, Local binary rigidity, together with its preceding outsider-state construction", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Almost every palette has a unique outsider, defining a measurable state \\( s(e)\\in\\{X,Y\\},\\qquad s(Re)\\ne s(e)\\text{ almost everywhere}. \\) If \\(e_0,Re_0\\notin\\mathcal B\\), then \\(s\\) is almost everywhere constant on an …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the triangle setting of Proposition 15.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Almost every palette has a unique outsider, defining a measurable state\n\\[\ns(e)\\in\\{X,Y\\},\\qquad s(Re)\\ne s(e)\\text{ almost everywhere}.\n\\]\nIf \\(e_0,Re_0\\notin\\mathcal B\\), then \\(s\\) is almost everywhere constant on an angular neighborhood of \\(e_0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>At any direction outside \\(\\mathcal B\\), two of the three signs agree. Those edges cover all triangle labels. Proposition 12 therefore gives a one-sided strip restricted to \\(X,Y\\), and an outsider in the palette almost everywhere. Two outsiders in one palette would leave none in the palette at \\(Re\\), contrary to the same conclusion and disjointness. This gives the unique alternating state.</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal U,\\mathcal V\\) be the two-label open strips near \\(e_0,Re_0\\), possibly on different radial sides. Set \\(z_0=x+e_0\\). There are two nonparallel unit directions \\(v'_1,v'_2\\) with \\(z_0+v'_j\\in\\mathcal V\\). Rotate \\(e_0=1\\) and use\n\\[\nF(r,\\theta)=|re^{i\\theta}-1|^2-1=r^2-2r\\cos\\theta.\n\\]\nAt \\((1,\\pi/3)\\), \\(F=0\\), \\(\\partial_\\theta F=\\sqrt3\\ne0\\). The implicit function theorem moves \\(r\\) onto either desired side inside \\(\\mathcal V\\); openness supplies two nonparallel directions.</p><p>Take a smaller connected polar product strip \\(\\mathcal U'\\subset\\mathcal U\\), approaching radius one, such that \\(q+v'_j\\in\\mathcal V\\) for all \\(q\\in\\mathcal U'\\).</p><p>Locally, any sufficiently small shift is\n\\[\nh=t_1Jv'_1+t_2Jv'_2.\n\\]\nMove by these terms successively. For either move from \\(q'\\) to \\(q'+tJv'\\), the common unit neighbor is\n\\[\nq'+\\frac t2Jv'+\\sqrt{1-t^2/4}\\,v'\\in\\mathcal V.\n\\]\nSmallness keeps all endpoints in \\(\\mathcal U\\), all common neighbors in \\(\\mathcal V\\), and \\(|t|&lt;2\\). This gives four unit edges.</p><p>For each fixed permissible \\(h\\), all five vertices are translations of \\(q\\); almost every starting point makes them typical and two-labeled. Properness forces alternation, hence \\(c(q)=c(q+h)\\). Joint measurability and Fubini give this for almost every \\((q,h)\\). Changing variables to \\((q,q+h)\\) proves that almost every ordered pair in a smaller ball has equal colors, so \\(c\\) is locally essentially constant.</p><p>Points with local essential color \\(X\\), respectively \\(Y\\), form disjoint open sets covering connected \\(\\mathcal U'\\). Only one is nonempty; a countable neighborhood subcover makes the whole strip essentially monochromatic.</p><p>Choose typical radial samplings approaching one from that side. Polar Fubini makes their arc samples equal that color almost everywhere. A weak-* trace puts it into the palette on the arc. It is the unique outsider, so \\(s\\) is locally essentially constant.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.2", "orig": "Lemma 8.7, The boundary is one orbit", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The set \\(E_s\\) is exactly one six-position \\(R\\)-orbit.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the outsider state \\(s\\) from Lemma 15.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Its essential boundary \\(E_s\\) consists of directions where \\(s\\) is not almost everywhere constant on any open neighborhood.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The set \\(E_s\\) is exactly one six-position \\(R\\)-orbit. Its complementary sectors have angle \\(\\pi/3\\), with alternating essential values \\(X,Y\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The set \\(E_s\\) is closed. It is nonempty, since otherwise local constancy, connectedness, and a countable subcover would make \\(s\\) globally constant, contrary to the flip. The flip also makes \\(E_s\\) \\(R\\)-invariant.</p><p>For a boundary orbit, Lemma 15.1 says each consecutive pair has at least one vertex in \\(\\mathcal B\\). Modulo antipodes its three positions form a triangle, whose edges must be covered by projective classes of \\(\\mathcal B\\). At least two are needed. There are at most three such classes, one per interface direction. Distinct \\(R\\)-orbits have disjoint projective positions, since an antipode is already the third rotation. Two boundary orbits would require at least four classes. Hence there is exactly one.</p><p>Each complementary open sector has one essential value by local constancy and connectedness. Rotation carries it to the next and flips the value.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.3", "orig": "Lemma 8.8, Polynomial three-label region", "depth": 1, "snippet": "If \\(0<l<4\\) and \\( P:=\\upsilon^2(3\\xi^2-\\upsilon^2)^2 < P':=l^3(1-l/4)(l-1)^2, \\) then a neighborhood of \\(x+z\\) uses only the triangle labels up to a plane-null set.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Rotate coordinates about \\(x\\) so the sector boundaries in Lemma 15.2 are multiples of \\(\\pi/3\\). The outsider state is determined, in either order, by the sign of \\(\\sin(3\\theta)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  z=\\xi+i\\upsilon,\\qquad l=\\xi^2+\\upsilon^2.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If \\(0&lt;l&lt;4\\) and\n\\[\nP:=\\upsilon^2(3\\xi^2-\\upsilon^2)^2\n&lt;\nP':=l^3(1-l/4)(l-1)^2,\n\\]\nthen a neighborhood of \\(x+z\\) uses only the triangle labels up to a plane-null set.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(z=re^{i\\phi}\\), \\(0&lt;r&lt;2\\), and \\(\\delta=\\arccos(r/2)\\in(0,\\pi/2)\\). The nonparallel directions with angles \\(\\phi-\\delta,\\phi+\\delta\\) sum to \\(z\\).</p><p>Triple-angle identities give\n\\[\n\\sin^2(3\\phi)=\\frac{\\upsilon^2(3\\xi^2-\\upsilon^2)^2}{l^3},\n\\qquad\n\\sin(3\\delta)=\\sqrt{1-l/4}(l-1),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\sin(3(\\phi-\\delta))\\sin(3(\\phi+\\delta))\n=\\sin^2(3\\phi)-\\sin^2(3\\delta)&lt;0.\n\\]\nThe endpoints lie strictly inside sectors with opposite outsider states. Choose open arcs within those sectors. Palette exclusion removes both outsiders at almost every sum. The sum map is a local diffeomorphism, with Jacobian \\(\\sin(2\\delta)\\ne0\\), so change of variables transfers product-null exceptions to spatial null exceptions on an open neighborhood of \\(z\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.4", "orig": "Lemma 8.9, Seven-point certificate", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every \\(z_g\\) satisfies \\(0<|z_g|^2<4\\) and \\(P<P'\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal M=\\{0,A,B,T,uA,uB,uT\\},\n  \\]\n  where\n  \\[\n  A=\\frac{\\sqrt3+i}{2},\\qquad\n  B=\\frac{\\sqrt3-i}{2},\\qquad\n  T=\\sqrt3,\\qquad\n  u=\\frac{5+i\\sqrt{11}}6.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(g\\in\\mathcal M\\), define\n  \\[\n  z_g=\\frac{35+12i}{37}\n  \\left(g+\\frac{-290+149i}{250}\\right).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For each \\(z_g=\\xi+i\\upsilon\\), use \\(l,P,P'\\) from Lemma 15.3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every \\(z_g\\) satisfies \\(0&lt;|z_g|^2&lt;4\\) and \\(P&lt;P'\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>All terminating decimals below are exact rationals. Coordinate centers and bounds are\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|rr|rr}\ng&amp;\\xi_0&amp;\\upsilon_0&amp;P_+&amp;P'_-\\\\ \\hline\n0&amp;-1.2906&amp;0.1876&amp;0.90&amp;1.35\\\\\nA&amp;-0.6335&amp;0.9414&amp;0.095&amp;0.110\\\\\nB&amp;-0.3092&amp;-0.0045&amp;0.000002&amp;0.00065\\\\\nT&amp;0.3478&amp;0.7493&amp;0.023&amp;0.026\\\\\nuA&amp;-1.1598&amp;1.1790&amp;9.8&amp;19.0\\\\\nuB&amp;-0.3666&amp;0.5700&amp;0.0022&amp;0.024\\\\\nuT&amp;-0.2358&amp;1.5614&amp;12.65&amp;12.9\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nEach coordinate is within \\(h=0.00011\\) of its center.</p><p>Squaring rational endpoints gives\n\\[\n1.73205&lt;\\sqrt3&lt;1.73206,\\qquad\n3.31662&lt;\\sqrt{11}&lt;3.31663.\n\\]\nPut \\(a_*=1.732055\\), \\(b_*=3.316625\\), \\(\\epsilon=0.000005\\). Midpoint substitution changes their product by less than\n\\[\n(a_*+b_*)\\epsilon+\\epsilon^2\n=0.000025243425&lt;0.000026.\n\\]\nThe product coordinates are\n\\[\nuA=\\left(\\frac{5\\sqrt3-\\sqrt{11}}{12},\n\\frac{\\sqrt{33}+5}{12}\\right),\n\\]\n\\[\nuB=\\left(\\frac{5\\sqrt3+\\sqrt{11}}{12},\n\\frac{\\sqrt{33}-5}{12}\\right),\n\\qquad\nuT=\\left(\\frac{5\\sqrt3}{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{33}}6\\right).\n\\]\nThus each unplaced coordinate changes by at most \\(\\epsilon\\). The placement rotation has absolute row sum \\(47/37\\).</p><p>If \\(\\widetilde z_g\\) denotes midpoint substitution, direct rational substitution yields\n\\[\n\\widetilde z_g-(\\xi_0+i\\upsilon_0)\n=\\frac{d_x+id_y}{710400000000},\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|rr}\ng&amp;d_x&amp;d_y\\\\ \\hline\n0&amp;3840000&amp;-23040000\\\\\nA&amp;-29520000&amp;10176000\\\\\nB&amp;-12240000&amp;-22464000\\\\\nT&amp;25440000&amp;10752000\\\\\nuA&amp;32043244&amp;-19212795\\\\\nuB&amp;4763244&amp;-27212795\\\\\nuT&amp;32966488&amp;-23385590\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nEvery magnitude is less than \\(35520000=710400000000/20000\\). Hence final coordinate errors are at most\n\\[\n\\frac{47}{37}\\epsilon+0.00005&lt;0.00011=h.\n\\]</p><p>Set\n\\[\nx_l=(|\\xi_0|-h)^2,\\quad x_u=(|\\xi_0|+h)^2,\\qquad\ny_l=(|\\upsilon_0|-h)^2,\\quad y_u=(|\\upsilon_0|+h)^2,\n\\]\n\\[\nl_l=x_l+y_l,\\quad l_u=x_u+y_u,\\qquad\nH=\\max\\{|3x_l-y_u|,\\ |3x_u-y_l|\\}.\n\\]\nAll absolute coordinate centers exceed \\(h\\). The following rational factors satisfy\n\\(y_u&lt;b_y\\), \\(H&lt;b_H\\), and \\(a&lt;l_l\\le l_u&lt;b\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|rrrr}\ng&amp;b_y&amp;b_H&amp;a&amp;b\\\\ \\hline\n0&amp;0.0353&amp;4.963&amp;1.70&amp;1.71\\\\\nA&amp;0.887&amp;0.319&amp;1.28&amp;1.29\\\\\nB&amp;0.000022&amp;0.287&amp;0.095&amp;0.096\\\\\nT&amp;0.562&amp;0.199&amp;0.682&amp;0.683\\\\\nuA&amp;1.391&amp;2.647&amp;2.73&amp;2.74\\\\\nuB&amp;0.326&amp;0.079&amp;0.459&amp;0.46\\\\\nuT&amp;2.44&amp;2.273&amp;2.493&amp;2.495\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThese comparisons require only integer squaring: with \\(M=10^5\\), \\(m=M|\\xi_0|\\), \\(n=M|\\upsilon_0|\\),\n\\[\n(x_l,x_u,y_l,y_u)\n=10^{-10}\\bigl((m-11)^2,(m+11)^2,(n-11)^2,(n+11)^2\\bigr).\n\\]</p><p>Each interval \\((a,b)\\) lies in \\((0,4)\\) and avoids one. Therefore\n\\[\nP\\le y_uH^2&lt;b_yb_H^2&lt;P_+,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nP'\\ge l_l^3(1-l_u/4)\n\\min\\{|l_l-1|,|l_u-1|\\}^2\n&gt;\na^3(1-b/4)\\min\\{|a-1|,|b-1|\\}^2&gt;P'_-.\n\\]\nThe last comparisons are direct rational products. For the final row,\n\\[\n2.44(2.273)^2&lt;12.65,\\qquad\n(2.493)^3(1-2.495/4)(1.493)^2&gt;12.9.\n\\]\nThe other rows give their displayed bounds by the same products. Since \\(P_+&lt;P'_-\\) in every row, the strict polynomial inequalities and radius conditions follow.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 16", "orig": "Theorem 1.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is no weak measurable five-coloring of the Euclidean plane.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use weak measurable colorings as defined in Theorem 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is no weak measurable five-coloring of the Euclidean plane.</p>", "proof": "<p>A weak measurable five-coloring would, by Proposition 11, produce an interface cycle of length three, four, or five with the stated common-center and exclusion properties. Propositions 13, 14, and 15 exclude all three possibilities.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 2, "cite"], [1, 2, "cite"], [1, 53, "cite"], [3, 13, "cite"], [4, 5, "cite"], [4, 7, "cite"], [5, 3, "cite"], [5, 7, "cite"], [6, 7, "cite"], [7, 8, "cite"], [7, 10, "cite"], [8, 10, "cite"], [9, 10, "cite"], [10, 3, "cite"], [11, 3, "cite"], [12, 3, "cite"], [13, 0, "cite"], [13, 1, "cite"], [14, 13, "cite"], [15, 1, "cite"], [15, 17, "cite"], [15, 28, "cite"], [16, 15, "cite"], [17, 21, "cite"], [17, 24, "cite"], [17, 25, "cite"], [17, 28, "cite"], [17, 29, "cite"], [17, 30, "cite"], [18, 17, "cite"], [19, 17, "cite"], [19, 20, "cite"], [20, 17, "cite"], [21, 24, "cite"], [21, 30, "cite"], [22, 21, "cite"], [22, 23, "cite"], [23, 21, "cite"], [24, 31, "cite"], [25, 30, "cite"], [25, 31, "cite"], [26, 34, "cite"], [27, 25, "cite"], [28, 32, "cite"], [28, 34, "cite"], [28, 46, "cite"], [29, 31, "cite"], [29, 45, "cite"], [31, 32, "cite"], [32, 30, "cite"], [33, 34, "cite"], [34, 30, "cite"], [35, 43, "cite"], [35, 44, "cite"], [35, 45, "cite"], [35, 48, "cite"], [35, 53, "cite"], [36, 37, "cite"], [36, 40, "cite"], [37, 38, "cite"], [38, 35, "cite"], [38, 40, "cite"], [39, 40, "cite"], [40, 35, "cite"], [41, 35, "cite"], [41, 42, "cite"], [42, 35, "cite"], [43, 45, "cite"], [43, 48, "cite"], [43, 49, "cite"], [44, 53, "cite"], [45, 53, "cite"], [46, 45, "cite"], [46, 47, "cite"], [47, 45, "cite"], [48, 53, "cite"], [49, 50, "cite"], [50, 51, "cite"], [51, 48, "cite"], [51, 52, "cite"], [52, 48, "cite"], [53, 0, "cite"]]});
